OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026", "title": "Essential Singularity of the Correlation Length in the Planar XY Model", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "216", "family_title": "Critical and near-critical XY scaling and BKT universality", "abstract_html": "We prove the Berezinskii–Kosterlitz–Thouless essential singularity for the correlation length of the nearest-neighbor cosine XY model on the square lattice. Let \\(m(b)\\) be the mass obtained by taking first the free-box thermodynamic limit and then the separation limit along a coordinate axis. As the inverse temperature <i>b</i> approaches <i>b</i><sub><i>c</i></sub> from the massive side,", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a constant \\(A_{\\mathrm{XY}}\\in(0,\\infty)\\) such that \\( \\lim_{b\\uparrow b_c}\\sqrt{b_c-b}\\,\\log\\xi(b)=A_{\\mathrm{XY}}. \\) The temperature parameter is exactly the coefficient \\(b\\) in the cosine interaction.", "statement": "<p>**Essential singularity of the correlation length — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For \\(R\\in\\mathbb N\\), let \\(\\Lambda_R=[-R,R]^2\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\), and let \\(E_R\\) contain each unordered nearest-neighbor edge of \\(\\Lambda_R\\) once.</p><p class=\"li\">At inverse temperature \\(b&gt;0\\), the free-boundary planar XY measure is\n  \\[\n  \\frac1{Z_{R,b}}\n  \\exp\\!\\left(b\\sum_{\\{u,v\\}\\in E_R}\\cos(\\theta_u-\\theta_v)\\right)\n  \\prod_{u\\in\\Lambda_R}\\frac{d\\theta_u}{2\\pi},\n  \\qquad \\theta_u\\in\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z).\n  \\]\n  There are no exterior edges.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(e_1=(1,0)\\), and define\n  \\[\n  C_b(r)=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\n  \\mathbb E^{\\mathrm{free}}_{R,b}\\cos(\\theta_0-\\theta_{re_1}),\n  \\qquad\n  m(b)=\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}-\\frac1r\\log C_b(r),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  b_c=\\inf\\{b&gt;0:m(b)=0\\}.\n  \\]\n  The thermodynamic limit precedes the separation limit.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the standard existence of these limits and the facts\n  \\[\n  0&lt;b_c&lt;\\infty,\\qquad m(b_c)=0,\\qquad\n  0&lt;m(b)&lt;\\infty\\quad(0&lt;b&lt;b_c).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">In the massive phase, set \\(\\xi(b)=m(b)^{-1}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a constant \\(A_{\\mathrm{XY}}\\in(0,\\infty)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\lim_{b\\uparrow b_c}\\sqrt{b_c-b}\\,\\log\\xi(b)=A_{\\mathrm{XY}}.\n\\]\nThe temperature parameter is exactly the coefficient \\(b\\) in the cosine interaction. The constant depends on this normalization and is not evaluated in closed form.</p>", "proof": "<p>Start with any sequence \\(b_k&lt;b_c\\) tending to \\(b_c\\), and put\n\\(\\delta_k=\\sqrt{b_c-b_k}\\). Propositions \\(8\\)–\\(10\\) furnish a subsequence with the step profile whose transition point is\n\\[\nT=\\frac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{3\\mathcal P}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal P\\in(0,\\infty)\\), constructed in Proposition \\(6\\), is independent of the extracted sequence. Proposition \\(7\\) identifies this point through the small-activity passage time and exit variance deficit.</p><p>Propositions \\(11\\) and \\(13\\) give, on that subsequence,\n\\[\n\\delta_k\\log_L\\frac1{m(b_k)}\\longrightarrow T.\n\\]\nIf this limit failed for the original parameter approach, there would be a sequence staying a fixed positive distance from \\(T\\). Applying the same extraction to that sequence would contradict the subsequential conclusion. Hence the limit holds as \\(b\\uparrow b_c\\), without extraction.</p><p>Converting to natural logarithms,\n\\[\n\\lim_{b\\uparrow b_c}\\sqrt{b_c-b}\\log\\frac1{m(b)}\n=(\\log L)T\n=\\frac{\\pi\\log L}{\\sqrt{3\\mathcal P}}\n=:A_{\\mathrm{XY}}\\in(0,\\infty).\n\\]\nAll spin correlations used are free-box thermodynamic limits before the separation limit. Thus this is exactly the mass and correlation length defined in Theorem \\(1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The finite laws satisfy the comparison and reference bounds in Lemmas \\(1.1\\)–\\(1.2\\), the factorial-insertion and parameter-continuity estimates in Lemmas \\(1.3\\)–\\(1.4\\), and the strict temperature-response estimate …", "statement": "<p>**Finite height comparisons, analytic temperature dependence, and strict response.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G=(V,E)\\) be a finite primary height graph. Orient its edges once and write\n  \\(\\nabla_eh=h_{e^+}-h_{e^-}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Heights have spacing \\(2\\pi\\). Each unoriented edge contributes one factor\n  \\[\n  p_b(k)=e^{-b}I_k(b),\n  \\qquad\n  \\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}p_b(k)e^{tk}\n  =\\exp\\{b(\\cosh t-1)\\}.\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(p_b\\) is the law of the difference of two independent Poisson variables of mean \\(b/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize the primary Laplacian by\n  \\[\n  \\langle v,A_Gv\\rangle=\\sum_{e\\in E}(\\nabla_ev)^2.\n  \\]\n  All discrete pairings use counting measure.</p><p class=\"li\">A free connected component is taken modulo its common \\(2\\pi\\mathbb Z\\)-translations. A representative may be pinned at one vertex for invariant tests. If observations confine the constant, sum all its translates.</p><p class=\"li\">On a torus, use single-valued periodic heights, hence zero winding, on\n  \\(2\\pi\\mathbb Z^V/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z\\mathbf1)\\). Mean-zero representatives are projections of this lattice onto the mean-zero space.</p><p class=\"li\">Centered laws may include nonnegative quadratic penalties in real linear observations, independent Gaussian observation noises, and centered linear equalities. Affine laws have the same precision and parallel constraint space but may have an integral edge connection, nonzero equality values, or shifted observation centers.</p><p class=\"li\">For a mean-zero test \\(f\\), define its reference cost by\n  \\[\n  G_G(f)=\\langle f,A_G^+f\\rangle.\n  \\]\n  Equivalently, this is the minimum of \\(\\sum_e w_e^2\\) over flows representing\n  \\(\\langle h,f\\rangle=\\sum_e w_e\\nabla_eh\\). With primary pins, use the grounded Laplacian.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The finite laws satisfy the comparison and reference bounds in Lemmas \\(1.1\\)–\\(1.2\\), the factorial-insertion and parameter-continuity estimates in Lemmas \\(1.3\\)–\\(1.4\\), and the strict temperature-response estimate in Lemma \\(1.5\\). In particular, local centered normalizers are nonzero on complex parameter disks of radius proportional to the reciprocal number of primary edges, and nonzero mean-zero height tests have strictly positive variance response.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(1.1\\)–\\(1.2\\) give the finite comparisons and graph-uniform reference estimates. Lemmas \\(1.3\\)–\\(1.4\\) give analytic control on disks of radius proportional to \\(M^{-1}\\), using only local scalar normalizers. Lemma \\(1.5\\) supplies the strict response needed later to distinguish temperature variation from motion along the critical trajectory.</p><p>Fourier expansion of the free spin interaction gives the divergence-free integer-current sum. Rotating currents to dual edges gives the height law with exterior-face height zero. A two-spin insertion gives opposite unit divergences; subtracting a unit current along a joining path gives an integral connection on crossed dual edges. This is the free-boundary duality of [14, Lemma 2.1]. The factors \\(e^b\\) cancel in partition ratios.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Increasing centered precision decreases centered covariances and real moment generating functions.", "statement": "<p>**Finite comparisons — original Lemma 2.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the finite centered and affine height laws of Proposition \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li\">Increasing centered precision decreases centered covariances and real moment generating functions.</p><p class=\"li\">The covariance in an affine law, also after a real linear tilt, dominates the corresponding centered covariance.</p><p class=\"li\">A shifted-to-centered partition ratio is at most one and decreases when the same positive precision is added to both laws. This monotonicity also holds with a fixed real linear statistic inserted in the exponent of the affine numerator.</p><p class=\"li\">Centered invariant characteristic coefficients are positive and increase with precision. The Hessian of their logarithm at any phase is bounded below by its Hessian at zero.</p><p class=\"li\">On a freely translated component, a characteristic test is invariant when its coefficient sum is an integer; a real linear test must have coefficient sum zero.</p><p class=\"li\">For a centered real linear test \\(X\\),\n  \\[\n  \\log\\mathbb Ee^X\\ge\\frac12\\operatorname{Var}(X).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">As \\(b\\) increases, centered covariances and real transforms increase, whereas centered invariant characteristic coefficients decrease.</p>", "proof": "<p>The underlying lattice-Gaussian inequality is due to Regev and Stephens-Davidowitz [16]; related extensions to annealed integer-valued Gaussian fields appear in [1, Appendix D]. The fixed-\\(b\\) statements are [14, Lemma 2.2].</p><p>Replace each primary edge by \\(N\\) bonds in series, with normalized lattice-Gaussian bond weight \\((b/(2N))^{k^2}\\). On compact sets of real \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sum_k(b/(2N))^{k^2}e^{tk}}\n     {\\sum_k(b/(2N))^{k^2}}\n=\n1+\\frac bN(\\cosh t-1)+O(N^{-2}).\n\\]\nThe full chain converges to the Bessel increment law, including every fixed exponential moment.</p><p>Matching endpoints, cycle constraints, period constraints, and additional observations are positive quadratic forms or limits of such forms on the enlarged lattice. Apply the finite lattice-Gaussian comparison inequalities there. Increasing \\(b\\) decreases the bond precision \\(2\\log(2N/b)\\), proving the temperature monotonicities before passage to the limit.</p><p>A spanning-forest parametrization and full-chain exponential moments dominate the sums on each fixed primary graph. This justifies the limit, including polynomial insertions and real tilts. Confined constants are dominated by their observation penalties; unconfined constants are factored out.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(b\\in I\\) and each fixed \\(r\\ge1\\), \\( \\operatorname{Var}(X)\\le C_I\\sum_e w_e^2, \\qquad \\mathbb E|X|^r\\le C_{I,r}\\left(\\sum_e w_e^2\\right)^{r/2}. \\) For every fixed \\(t\\), the transform …", "statement": "<p>**Uniform reference bounds — original Lemma 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a compact interval \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X=\\sum_e w_e\\nabla_eh\\) in any centered finite law of Proposition \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(b\\in I\\) and each fixed \\(r\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(X)\\le C_I\\sum_e w_e^2,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E|X|^r\\le C_{I,r}\\left(\\sum_e w_e^2\\right)^{r/2}.\n\\]\nFor every fixed \\(t\\), the transform\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{tX}{(\\sum_e w_e^2)^{1/2}}\\right)\n\\]\nis uniformly bounded in the graph. More generally, if\n\\(\\max_e|tw_e|\\le K\\), then\n\\[\n\\log\\mathbb Ee^{tX}\\le C_{I,K}t^2\\sum_e w_e^2.\n\\]\nZero tests are interpreted separately.</p>", "proof": "<p>Release the centered endpoint, curl, period, and observation constraints in the full-chain construction of Lemma \\(1.1\\). The comparison bounds the transform by a product of independent whole-chain transforms. Their limiting logarithm is\n\\[\nb\\sum_e\\bigl(\\cosh(2\\pi tw_e)-1\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhich is bounded by \\(C_{I,K}t^2\\sum_e w_e^2\\) when the arguments are bounded. The same bound holds uniformly in sufficiently fine subdivision.</p><p>For a flow normalized by its Euclidean norm, every coefficient has modulus at most one. Fixed exponential moments then give the moment bounds and normalized-transform bound. This is the argument of [14, Lemma 2.3], with constants uniform on \\(I\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In a centered finite height law, including centered primary pins and observation penalties, \\( \\mathbb E_b(N)_\\ell\\le(bM)^\\ell. \\) If observation centers are prescribed real values, the unnormalized factorial insertion …", "statement": "<p>**Factorial insertions — original Lemma 2.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain independent Poisson variables \\(U_e,V_e\\) on each oriented edge and impose\n  \\(U_e-V_e=\\nabla_eh/(2\\pi)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The augmented unnormalized weight is\n  \\[\n  \\prod_{e\\in E}e^{-b}\\frac{(b/2)^{U_e+V_e}}{U_e!V_e!},\n  \\]\n  with the prescribed height constraints and observation penalties.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(N=\\sum_e(U_e+V_e)\\), \\(M=|E|\\), and\n  \\((N)_\\ell=N(N-1)\\cdots(N-\\ell+1)\\), with \\((N)_0=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>In a centered finite height law, including centered primary pins and observation penalties,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_b(N)_\\ell\\le(bM)^\\ell.\n\\]\nIf observation centers are prescribed real values, the unnormalized factorial insertion is bounded by \\((bM)^\\ell\\) times the corresponding zero-observation centered sum.</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand \\((N)_\\ell\\) over ordered choices of \\(\\ell\\) Poisson units, recording edges and signs. Removing those units leaves a partition sum with an integral edge connection and the same primary variables, pins, and observation penalties. The remaining Poisson differences obey the shifted curl and period constraints determined by that connection.</p><p>Each removed unit contributes \\(b/2\\). Lemma \\(1.1\\) bounds every shifted sum by its centered zero-observation counterpart. Summing the \\((2M)^\\ell\\) choices proves the assertion. Repeated choices of an edge cause no difficulty: falling factorials are exactly the factors canceled by removing several units from its Poisson factorial.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(c,C>0\\) such that, for \\(\\eta\\in K\\) and \\(|b-b_0|M\\le c\\), \\( \\frac{\\|\\mu_b^\\eta-\\mu_{b_0}^\\eta\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}}{Z_{b_0}^0} \\le C|b-b_0|M. \\) For complex weights, total variation means the sum of absolute …", "statement": "<p>**Finite-volume parameter continuity — original Lemma 2.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(b_0&gt;0\\) and a compact set \\(K\\) of complex observation vectors on a fixed finite list of observation shapes.</p><p class=\"li\">On a finite graph with \\(M\\) primary edges, let \\(\\mu_b^\\eta\\) be its unnormalized augmented weight and \\(Z_b^0\\) its centered zero-observation mass. Centered hard pins, if present, are included in both.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(c,C&gt;0\\) such that, for \\(\\eta\\in K\\) and \\(|b-b_0|M\\le c\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|\\mu_b^\\eta-\\mu_{b_0}^\\eta\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}}{Z_{b_0}^0}\n\\le C|b-b_0|M.\n\\]\nFor complex weights, total variation means the sum of absolute values. Constants may be uniform over a prescribed finite family of graph topologies and observation shapes. Each centered normalizer is nonzero on a disk of radius \\(c/M\\) about \\(b_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The augmented parameter likelihood is exactly\n\\[\ne^{-(b-b_0)M}(b/b_0)^N.\n\\]\nExpand the second factor in falling factorials. For real observations, Lemma \\(1.3\\) bounds the absolute sum of its positive-order terms, divided by \\(Z_{b_0}^0\\), by\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\ge1}\\frac{|b-b_0|^\\ell}{\\ell!b_0^\\ell}(b_0M)^\\ell\n=e^{|b-b_0|M}-1.\n\\]\nThe scalar exponential has the same bound.</p><p>For \\(\\eta=\\eta_1+i\\eta_2\\), the absolute observation penalty is its real-\\(\\eta_1\\) penalty multiplied by \\(e^{\\|\\eta_2\\|^2/2}\\), uniformly bounded on \\(K\\). This proves continuity. At zero observation, decrease \\(c\\) so that\n\\[\n|Z_b^0/Z_{b_0}^0-1|&lt;1/2,\n\\]\ngiving nonvanishing. The same expansion proves analyticity of all sums on compact parameter sets.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\frac{\\operatorname{Var}_b(X)^2}{4\\pi^2b^2G(f)} \\le\\partial_b\\operatorname{Var}_b(X) \\le C_I|E|G(f). \\) These inequalities hold on every zero-winding periodic torus, with constants independent of size.", "statement": "<p>**Temperature response — original Lemma 2.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be finite and connected, with centered Bessel height law.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f\\ne0\\) have coefficient sum zero, put\n  \\(X=\\langle h,f\\rangle\\), and let\n  \\(G(f)=\\langle f,A_G^+f\\rangle\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b\\) range over a compact interval \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac{\\operatorname{Var}_b(X)^2}{4\\pi^2b^2G(f)}\n\\le\\partial_b\\operatorname{Var}_b(X)\n\\le C_I|E|G(f).\n\\]\nThese inequalities hold on every zero-winding periodic torus, with constants independent of size.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(C_b\\) be the covariance operator on mean-zero tests. Removing one positive Poisson unit at edge \\(e\\) gives the shifted weight\n\\[\n\\prod_{d\\in E}p_b\\!\\left(\\nabla_dh/(2\\pi)-\\mathbf1_{\\{d=e\\}}\\right).\n\\]\nLet \\(r_e\\) be its partition ratio to the centered law, and \\(v_e\\) its mean of \\(X\\). Reflection \\(h\\mapsto-h\\) identifies the negative-removal law with its reflection. Thus its ratio is \\(r_e\\), its mean is \\(-v_e\\), and Lemma \\(1.1\\) gives\n\\(0&lt;r_e\\le1\\) and shifted variance at least \\(\\operatorname{Var}_b(X)\\).</p><p>Differentiation inserts \\(N/b\\); the deterministic score term \\(-|E|\\) cancels. Hence\n\\[\n\\partial_b\\operatorname{Var}_b(X)\n=b^{-1}\\operatorname{Cov}_b(X^2,N).\n\\]\nRemoving positive and negative units separately gives\n\\[\n\\partial_b\\operatorname{Var}_b(X)\n=\\sum_e r_e\\bigl(\\operatorname{Var}_{e,+}(X)\n-\\operatorname{Var}_b(X)+v_e^2\\bigr)\n\\ge\\sum_e r_ev_e^2.\n\\]\nThe same removal with insertion \\(X\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_b[(U_e-V_e)X]=br_ev_e,\n\\qquad\n\\nabla_e(C_bf)=2\\pi br_ev_e.\n\\]\nDiscrete integration by parts and Cauchy–Schwarz yield\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var}_b(X)^2\n&amp;=\\langle\\nabla A_G^+f,\\nabla C_bf\\rangle^2\\\\\n&amp;\\le G(f)\\sum_e|\\nabla_eC_bf|^2\\\\\n&amp;\\le4\\pi^2b^2G(f)\\sum_e r_ev_e^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis proves the lower bound.</p><p>For the upper bound, Lemma \\(1.3\\) gives\n\\(\\mathbb EN^2\\le(bM)^2+bM\\), where \\(M=|E|\\), while Lemma \\(1.2\\) gives\n\\(\\mathbb EX^4\\le C_IG(f)^2\\). Cauchy–Schwarz in the score identity gives\n\\[\n|\\partial_b\\operatorname{Var}_b(X)|\\le C_IMG(f).\n\\]\nFinite-volume analyticity justifies all differentiations. No inverse-precision bound for a subdivided chain is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every sufficiently small \\(\\rho>0\\), the fixed choices can be made so that constants \\(c_{R_0}>0\\), \\(s_0<\\infty\\) exist with the following property.", "statement": "<p>**Analytic initialization — original Proposition 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The critical free-height coefficient supplied by [14, Section 6] is\n  \\(a=8\\pi\\). Fix \\(\\alpha=\\sqrt a\\), \\(\\omega=2\\pi/\\alpha\\), and a reference field of covariance \\(A^+\\). Keep this reference fixed as \\(b\\) varies.</p><p class=\"li\">Partition the primary graph into whole square cells of side \\(s\\). Let \\(B_s\\) average over cells, and set\n  \\[\n  \\eta=B_sh+e,\n  \\]\n  where components of \\(e\\) are independent standard Gaussians.</p><p class=\"li\">For a retained graph \\(G\\), let \\(Z_{G,b}(\\eta)\\) be its height sum with observation penalty \\(e^{-\\|B_sh-\\eta\\|^2/2}\\), and define\n  \\[\n  E_G(\\eta)=\\min_v\\{a^{-1}\\|\\nabla v\\|_G^2+\\|B_sv-\\eta\\|^2\\}.\n  \\]\n  Each component constant is free in this minimization.</p><p class=\"li\">For a retained cell face \\(f=(Q,Q')\\), set \\(w_f=\\eta_{Q'}-\\eta_Q\\) and\n  \\[\n  \\chi_s(z)=\n  \\frac{\\int_{-s}^s e^{-(z-t)^2/2}\\,dt}\n       {\\int_{-s}^s e^{-t^2/2}\\,dt},\n  \\qquad\n  \\widehat Z_{G,b}(\\eta)=Z_{G,b}(\\eta)\\prod_f\\chi_s(w_f).\n  \\]\n  A deleted face loses its primary bonds and cutoff factor.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the reference split\n  \\[\n  P_u(\\cos t)=\\left(\\frac{\\sin(ut/2)}{u\\sin(t/2)}\\right)^{64},\n  \\qquad P_u=P_u(1-A/64),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  C_{&lt;u}=A^{-1}(I-P_u),\\quad\n  \\Gamma_u=A^{-1}(P_u-P_{Lu}),\\quad\n  C_{\\ge u}=A^+P_u.\n  \\]\n  Removable values and constant-mode polynomial continuations are understood. The first two covariances are positive and finite range. Their constant modes may be integrated because the final common mean is uniform modulo \\(\\omega\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A polymer \\(X\\) is a connected finite set of \\(u\\)-blocks. Field dependence is confined to its occupied support and the prescribed microscopic collar of [15, Proposition 4.1]. The norm padding \\(D_u(X)\\) is enlarged to whole observation cells and contains the fixed difference stencils.</p><p class=\"li\">Activities belong to the complex bulk symmetry class\n  \\[\n  K(X,-\\phi)=K(X,\\phi),\\quad\n  K(X,\\phi+\\omega\\mathbf1)=K(X,\\phi),\\quad\n  K(gX,g\\phi)=K(X,\\phi),\n  \\]\n  for compatible block translations and square-lattice symmetries; tori have the corresponding periodic rules.</p><p class=\"li\">Use\n  \\[\n  \\|f\\|_{u,D}=\\max_{v\\in D}\n  \\{|f(v)|,u|\\nabla f(v)|,u^2|\\nabla^2f(v)|\\},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  |K(X)|_{u,\\phi}\n  =\\sum_{r\\ge0}\\frac{h_0^r}{r!}\n  \\sup_{\\|f_i\\|_{u,D_u(X)}\\le1}\n  |D^rK(X,\\phi)[f_1,\\ldots,f_r]|.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(\\mathcal W_u^\\kappa\\) be exactly the ordinary regulator of [15, Section 4.2, (41)–(43)], and set\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal V_u(X,\\phi)=\\frac12\\sup_{\\zeta,e}\n  \\left\\{\n  h_*E_{D_u(X)}(B_s\\alpha(\\phi+\\zeta)+e)\n  -p_1^{-1}(\\|\\zeta\\|_{C_{&lt;u}}^2+\\|e\\|^2)\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\]\n  The Cameron norm is on the covariance support. With fixed \\(A_w&gt;1\\),\n  \\[\n  \\|K\\|_{u,s}\n  =\\sup_{X,\\phi}\n  A_w^{|X|}\n  \\frac{|K(X)|_{u,\\phi}}\n       {\\mathcal W_u^\\kappa(X,\\phi)e^{\\mathcal V_u(X,\\phi)}}.\n  \\]\n  Denote the resulting bulk activity space by \\(\\mathcal A^{\\mathrm{bulk}}_{u,s}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose the fixed parameters of [15] with strict reserves\n  \\[\n  1-c_*&lt;h_1&lt;h_*&lt;1/p_2,\\qquad1&lt;p_1&lt;p_2.\n  \\]\n  Choose a sufficiently large dyadic block ratio \\(L\\), coloring period \\(q\\), sufficiently large dyadic \\(R_0\\), and \\(P_0=R_0^5\\). An admissible \\(s\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  qR_0s=L^{j_0},\n  \\]\n  where \\(j_0\\) is the absolute entry index.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the finite initialization of [15] with no magnetic or compulsory component. Its partition functional is\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal Z_u(K;\\phi)\n  =\\sum_{V\\subset\\mathcal B_u}\\prod_{X\\in\\operatorname{Comp}(V)}K(X,\\phi).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently small \\(\\rho&gt;0\\), the fixed choices can be made so that constants \\(c_{R_0}&gt;0\\), \\(s_0&lt;\\infty\\) exist with the following property. For every admissible \\(s\\ge s_0\\), the plane and compatible-torus entry activities \\(K_s(b)\\) are Banach analytic on\n\\[\n|b-b_c|&lt;c_{R_0}s^{-2},\n\\]\ntake values in the corresponding activity space, and have norm at most \\(\\rho\\). They are real on real fields for real \\(b\\). Their support, symmetry, and local copying identities are unchanged throughout the disk. For real parameters, the cutoff partition representation is exact, up to a field-independent scalar, after averaging \\(e\\) and \\(C_{&lt;L^{j_0}}\\). On a smaller concentric disk,\n\\[\n\\|\\partial_b^rK_s(b)\\|_{L^{j_0},s}\\le C_{R_0,r}s^{2r},\n\\qquad r\\ge1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>**Large-field reserve.** Adjacent-cell averages have primary flow representations with coefficient bound \\(C/s\\) and squared cost \\(C\\). More explicitly, straight transports to a neighboring cell give flows \\(\\Phi_f\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\max_e\\left|\\sum_f t_f\\Phi_f(e)\\right|\n\\le Cs^{-1}\\max_f|t_f|,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_e\\left|\\sum_f t_f\\Phi_f(e)\\right|^2\\le C\\sum_f t_f^2.\n\\]\nApply Proposition \\(1\\)'s transform bound with tilts up to a fixed multiple of \\(s\\), and use the Gaussian tails of \\(\\chi_s\\). Uniformly for real \\(b\\) in a compact neighborhood of \\(b_c\\),\n\\[\ne^{E_G(\\eta)/2}\\frac{\\widehat Z_{G,b}(\\eta)}{Z_{G,b}(0)}\n\\le e^{(1-c_*)E_G(\\eta)/2},\n\\qquad \\eta\\in\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal Q(G)}.\n\\]\nThe tilted affine comparison also applies at prescribed observations. Releasing the height and noise equalities gives \\(e^{C\\sum_ft_f^2}\\). Choose \\(t_f\\) proportional to\n\\(\\operatorname{sgn}(w_f)\\min(|w_f|,s)\\); for \\(|w_f|&gt;s\\), the cutoff supplies the remaining quadratic decay. Cell interpolation gives\n\\(E_G(\\eta)\\le C\\sum_fw_f^2\\). These are [15, Lemma 4.4 and (57)], whose physical transform is uniform on the chosen \\(b\\)-interval.</p><p>**Finite algebra and patterns.** Keep the geometry and algebra of [15, Sections 4.6–4.7, Theorem 4.6], changing only physical weights and scalar normalizers. A finite colored sequence isolates tiles of side comparable to \\(R_0\\). For open and cut patches \\(o_i,c_i\\), use\n\\[\nB_i(b,\\eta)=C_i(b)e^{-(E_{o_i}(\\eta)-E_{c_i}(\\eta))/2},\n\\qquad\nC_i(b)=\\frac{Z_{o_i,b}(0)}{Z_{c_i,b}(0)}.\n\\]\nWrite each original factor as its proxied cut factor plus their difference. Choosing the difference marks the event; subsequent nearby events are skipped. Connected marked neighborhoods form halos. Outside halos, isolated tiles contribute one or their normalized factor minus one; the latter are selected tiles.</p><p>Expand the remaining Gaussian mismatch in links \\(e^{b_\\ell w_fw_{f'}}-1\\), with\n\\[\n|b_\\ell|\\le Ce^{-c(R_0+d_\\ell)}.\n\\]\nThe tagged path length \\(d_\\ell\\) includes winding on a torus, and a link covers at most \\(C(1+d_\\ell/R_0)\\) entry blocks.</p><p>For a pattern \\(\\pi\\), let \\(l(\\pi)\\) count marks and selected tiles, and let \\(X(\\pi)\\) be its complete occupied cover. Each physical item has at most \\(C_{R_0}\\) observation cells and \\(C_{R_0}s^2\\) primary edges. Link-only cells have no physical factor. Cancel identical factors outside affected halos before defining the pattern. Denominators are products of affected local \\(C_i(b)\\)'s and isolated-tile normalizers. No centered partition function of an arbitrarily large halo is a defining denominator. Keep all Gaussian energies fixed.</p><p>**Real-parameter smallness.** The critical positive expansion uses\n\\[\nd_i(\\eta,\\gamma)\n=\\frac{|K_{o_i}(\\eta,\\gamma)-B_i(\\eta)K_{c_i}(\\eta,\\gamma)|}\n       {K_{o_i}(\\eta,\\gamma)+B_i(\\eta)K_{c_i}(\\eta,\\gamma)}\n\\le1,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\gamma\\) prescribes guard heights. Common exterior factors cancel. Cut halos into \\(P_0\\)-boxes and retain only guards tested inside a box. The affine comparisons, zero-guard ratios, separated-pin comparisons, and small Gaussian seam errors are exactly the positive-topology accounting of [15, (71)–(74)].</p><p>After fixed geometric and field parameters are chosen, the compact-data argument uses finitely many topologies, each with at most \\(C_{R_0}s^2\\) primary edges. A good box has bounded face-square sum; period recentering puts its observations in a fixed compact set. The critical inputs [15, Proposition 2.3 and (75)–(80)] give positive-factor bounds, separated-pin restoration, and the finite list of moments of \\(d_i\\), with strict slack.</p><p>Lemma \\(1.4\\) preserves these tolerances for\n\\(|b-b_c|\\le c_{R_0}s^{-2}\\). Apply it separately to pinned sums, never treating an absolute unpinned error as a relative estimate for a rare pin event. On compact real observations,\n\\[\n\\frac{Z_{G,b}(\\eta)}{Z_{G,b}(0)}\n=e^{-\\|\\eta\\|^2/2}\\mathbb E_{G,b,0}e^{\\langle\\eta,B_sh\\rangle}\n\\ge e^{-\\|\\eta\\|^2/2},\n\\]\nconverting normalized total variation to relative control.</p><p>For growing guard arrays, compare complete adjacent positive box weights \\(A_b(\\gamma),B_b(\\gamma)\\), including proxies and cutoffs. Their masses are uniformly comparable by open and zero-guard brackets, separated-pin bounds, and the preceding lower bound. For nonnegative \\(A,B,A',B'\\),\n\\[\n(A+B)\\left|\n\\frac{|A-B|}{A+B}-\\frac{|A'-B'|}{A'+B'}\n\\right|\n\\le2(|A-A'|+|B-B'|).\n\\]\nFor the \\(r\\)-th power, multiply the bound by at most \\(r\\). Hence all required fixed moments vary by a quantity tending to zero with \\(c_{R_0}\\); no pointwise guard estimate is needed. The complex-observation version of Lemma \\(1.4\\) preserves compact imaginary-observation estimates.</p><p>The finite comparisons and large-field reserve control noncompact boxes with the same reserves. Gaussian localization, links, and counting are independent of \\(b\\). Repeating [15, Lemma 4.7, (85)–(89)] gives, on field disks larger than the polarization radius,\n\\[\n|F_\\pi(b;\\phi+zf)|\n\\le C^{l(\\pi)}e^{-cR_0l(\\pi)}\n\\prod_{\\ell\\in\\pi}Ce^{-c(R_0+d_\\ell)}\n\\,\\mathcal W^\\kappa_{L^{j_0}}(X,\\phi)\ne^{\\mathcal V_{L^{j_0}}(X,\\phi)}\n\\]\nfor real \\(b\\) in the interval, real \\(\\phi\\), and normalized \\(f\\).</p><p>**Complex majorant in the same norm.** Put \\(r_s=c_{R_0}s^{-2}\\), \\(b_+=b_c+r_s\\). For \\(|b-b_c|\\le r_s\\), \\(|b|\\le b_+\\). On a branch with \\(N_\\pi\\) physical primary edges,\n\\[\n\\left|\\prod_e e^{-b}\\frac{(b/2)^{U_e+V_e}}{U_e!V_e!}\\right|\n\\le\ne^{(b_+-\\Re b)N_\\pi}\n\\prod_e e^{-b_+}\\frac{(b_+/2)^{U_e+V_e}}{U_e!V_e!}.\n\\]\nHere \\(N_\\pi\\le C_{R_0}l(\\pi)s^2\\). Every local scalar normalizer is nonzero by Lemma \\(1.4\\), and its value and inverse relative to \\(b_+\\) cost a fixed factor. There are \\(O(l(\\pi))\\) such scalars. Expand marked differences into two branches, compare unnormalized numerators first, then local scalars, and only then apply the real positive-topology estimate at \\(b_+\\). No complex large-halo normalizer is used.</p><p>On the fixed field disk, imaginary observation coordinates are bounded and cost \\(e^{C_{R_0}l(\\pi)}\\). Since\n\\[\n\\Re E_G(\\eta_1+i\\eta_2)=E_G(\\eta_1)-E_G(\\eta_2),\n\\]\nthe imaginary Gaussian terms have the same cost. Combine the real Gaussian shapes into the reference mismatch before bounding them; overlapping proxies do not create overlapping broad-energy charges.</p><p>Dropping the fractions \\(d_i\\), the broad physical cost is\n\\[\n\\frac{1-c_*}{2}\\sum_BE_B(\\eta_1).\n\\]\nThe actual halo pieces and selected tiles \\(B\\) are disjoint, and restriction of a trial field gives\n\\(\\sum_BE_B\\le E_D\\), \\(D=D_{L^{j_0}}(X)\\). Proxy, seam, and link errors have small face-square coefficients with bounded per-face sum. For \\(R_0\\) large, the cell-flow bound absorbs them into \\(h_1-(1-c_*)\\). Thus the full positive quadratic form satisfies\n\\[\nq_\\pi(\\eta_1)\\le\\frac12h_1E_D(\\eta_1).\n\\]</p><p>Write averaging variables as a standard Gaussian vector \\(\\zeta\\), with \\(\\eta_1=d+S\\zeta\\), and set\n\\(M_\\pi=\\nabla_\\zeta^2[p_1q_\\pi(d+S\\zeta)]\\). Then\n\\(0\\le M_\\pi\\le p_1h_1I&lt;I\\), and Gaussian completion gives\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\mathbb Ee^{p_1q_\\pi(d+S\\zeta)}\\bigr)^{1/p_1}\n=\n\\det(I-M_\\pi)^{-1/(2p_1)}\n\\exp\\sup_\\zeta\n\\left\\{q_\\pi(d+S\\zeta)-\\frac{\\|\\zeta\\|^2}{2p_1}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe supremum is bounded by the same observation regulator. Broad-energy rank is bounded by its number of physical observation coordinates. Link-only cells enter through their reserved small costs. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}M_\\pi\n\\le C_{R_0}l(\\pi)\n+C\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\pi}e^{-c(R_0+d_\\ell)}\\operatorname{poly}(d_\\ell).\n\\]\nA face contrast has bounded reference variance, including for long links. Since \\(\\|M_\\pi\\|\\) stays below one,\n\\(-\\log\\det(I-M_\\pi)\\le C\\operatorname{tr}M_\\pi\\).\nHölder handles the ordinary regulator with its fixed reserve. Bounded field translations cost \\(e^{C|X|}\\), absorbed by weakening link gains. Therefore\n\\[\n|F_\\pi(b;\\phi+zf)|\n\\le e^{C_{R_0}l(\\pi)}\n\\prod_{\\ell\\in\\pi}Ce^{-c(R_0+d_\\ell)}\n\\,\\mathcal W^\\kappa_{L^{j_0}}(X,\\phi)\ne^{\\mathcal V_{L^{j_0}}(X,\\phi)}.\n\\]\nThis domination also gives analyticity after Gaussian averaging.</p><p>**Recovering complex smallness.** Apply the two-constants theorem on upper and lower parameter half-disks. The real diameter has the small bound; the semicircle has the majorant. On \\(|b-b_c|\\le\\vartheta r_s\\), the semicircle's harmonic measure is at most \\(\\omega(\\vartheta)\\to0\\). Divide out the parameter-independent regulator and weakened link product. The physical-item factor becomes at most\n\\[\n\\exp\\left(\n[-(1-\\omega(\\vartheta))cR_0+\\omega(\\vartheta)C_{R_0}+C]l(\\pi)\n\\right).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\vartheta\\) so this is at most \\(e^{-cR_0l(\\pi)/2}\\). Link-only patterns already have their common gains.</p><p>Field Cauchy estimates and polarization sum all derivatives in the stated norm. Connected summation over complete occupied covers is the absolutely convergent sum of [15, Theorem 4.6], with norm at most \\(Ce^{-cR_0}\\), uniformly in \\(s\\), entry index, and compatible volume. Uniform convergence on compact parameter disks gives Banach analyticity. Absorb \\(\\vartheta\\) into \\(c_{R_0}\\), enlarge \\(R_0\\) to reach \\(\\rho\\), and apply parameter Cauchy estimates to obtain the derivative bounds.</p><p>The complete covers retain all field dependencies. Placement, coloring, Gaussian averaging, and field symmetries are unchanged. Exact finite algebra gives the real-parameter partition representation and local copying identities.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For all sufficiently large absolute \\(j\\), the map is analytic on a fixed ball \\( |t|+|z|+\\|\\mathcal R\\|_{L^j,s}<\\rho_{\\mathrm{map}} \\) and preserves the bulk symmetry class.", "statement": "<p>**Activity map — original Proposition 3.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed-reference activity construction, norms, support conventions, and symmetries of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At side \\(L^j\\), separate singleton functions\n  \\[\n  e_B^0(\\phi)=\\frac12\\sum_{v\\in B}\\sum_{e=\\pm e_1,\\pm e_2}\n  |\\nabla_e\\phi(v)|^2,\n  \\qquad\n  c_B^\\alpha(\\phi)=L^{-2j}\\sum_{v\\in B}\\cos(\\alpha\\phi_v).\n  \\]\n  Their coefficients are \\(t_j/2\\) and \\(z_j\\); the remaining activity is \\(\\mathcal R_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The exact map integrates \\(\\Gamma_{L^j}\\), removes designated field-independent factors, and reexpresses the result in the same subset-partition algebra.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large absolute \\(j\\), the map is analytic on a fixed ball\n\\[\n|t|+|z|+\\|\\mathcal R\\|_{L^j,s}&lt;\\rho_{\\mathrm{map}}\n\\]\nand preserves the bulk symmetry class. Its linear part is\n\\[\n(t,z,\\mathcal R)\\longmapsto\n(t+P_j^t\\mathcal R,\\lambda_jz+P_j^z\\mathcal R,C_j\\mathcal R),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\|C_j\\|\\le\\theta_0&lt;1,\\qquad\n\\lambda_j=1+O(e^{-cj}),\n\\]\nand the rows are uniformly bounded. Nonlinear terms and their Taylor derivatives have uniform quadratic bounds.</p><p>There are uniform analytic normal coordinates \\((u,Z,Q)\\) in which the pure Gaussian curve is \\((u,0,0)\\) and \\(u\\) is fixed along it. With\n\\[\nb_*=2\\log L,\\qquad H&gt;0,\\qquad\nx=b_*u,\\quad y=\\sqrt{b_*H}\\,Z,\\quad W=(x,y),\n\\]\nthe coordinate changes satisfy\n\\[\nt=u+O(u^2+\\|Q\\|),\\quad\nz=(1+O(e^{-cj}))Z+O(u^2+\\|Q\\|),\\quad\n\\mathcal R=O(u^2)+Q.\n\\]\nIn a fixed small tube,\n\\[\nW_+=W+(-y^2,-xy)+\\mathcal E_j(W,Q),\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\mathcal E_j(W,Q)|\n\\le C\\{j^{-2}|W|^2+|W|^3+(|W|+\\|Q\\|)\\|Q\\|\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|Q_+\\|\\le\\theta\\|Q\\|+C|W|^2,\\qquad\\theta&lt;1.\n\\]\nAll coordinate and map errors have the corresponding fixed-order differentiated analytic bounds.</p><p>On a separately initialized compatible torus with fixed range buffer, singleton coefficients copy the plane coefficients through the stop. Its remainder has its own contraction and quadratic analytic bound; its derivative at zero has no singleton forcing. Constants are uniform in stop and entry.</p><p>In equivalent scalar and remainder norms, for every prescribed \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\), the derivative of the full plane map has norm at most \\(1+\\gamma\\) on a sufficiently small tube. A driven torus remainder has the corresponding convolution growth bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>The exact local map and uniform norm bounds are [15, Proposition 4.1]; its triangular form is [15, (93)]. Field negation excludes sine singletons, and square symmetry makes the neutral quadratic isotropic.</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\ell_j=\\prod_{h\\ge j}\\lambda_h=1+O(e^{-cj}),\n\\qquad C_{h:j}=C_{h-1}\\cdots C_j.\n\\]\nThe balanced linear coordinates are\n\\[\nT=t+\\sum_{h\\ge j}P_h^tC_{h:j}\\mathcal R,\n\\qquad\nZ=\\ell_jz+\\sum_{h\\ge j}\\ell_{h+1}P_h^zC_{h:j}\\mathcal R.\n\\]\nThe series converge geometrically.</p><p>Initialize an exact Gaussian interaction\n\\(\\exp(d\\sum_Be_B^0/2)\\) at the chosen entry and iterate the same map. Its balanced gradient is\n\\(T_j^{\\mathrm g}(d)=d+O(d^2)\\), and its remainder is\n\\(\\mathcal R_j^{\\mathrm g}(d)=O(d^2)\\), uniformly on a common complex disk. Set\n\\[\nu=(T_j^{\\mathrm g})^{-1}(T),\n\\qquad\nQ=\\mathcal R-\\mathcal R_j^{\\mathrm g}(u).\n\\]\nThis is [15, (95), (99), (101)] and gives the coordinate bounds, with bounded linear remainder rows and \\(O(|u|)\\) derivatives of scalar quadratic terms.</p><p>The parity estimates of [15, Lemma 5.2] give leading scalar terms\n\\(-H_jZ^2\\) and \\(-b_juZ\\), with no nonconstant term on the Gaussian curve. Their differentiated remainders have the stated orders. By [15, Lemma 5.3],\n\\[\n|H_j-H|+|b_j-b_*|\\le C(1+j)^{-2}.\n\\]\nThe displayed rescaling gives the normal map. Small coefficients multiplying \\(Q\\) are absorbed into a slightly enlarged contraction. Copying and separate torus contraction belong to the same local-map statement; plane and torus remainders are not identified.</p><p>In balanced coordinates, scalar linear rows contain no remainder. Choose a product norm with the fixed remainder contraction. At zero, the derivative norm is at most one; uniform analytic derivative bounds give the \\(1+\\gamma\\) bound on a small tube. Convolution gives the torus growth assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "On a fixed small complex ball of terminal activities, the cutoff variance correction and phase expectation are analytic.", "statement": "<p>**Analytic torus observables and their physical comparisons — original Proposition 3.3, with original Lemmas 3.4–3.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Propositions \\(2\\)–\\(3\\). Fix a sufficiently large dyadic buffer \\(D_0\\), and for an absolute stop \\(j\\) use the torus of side\n  \\(n_j=D_0L^j\\), initialized separately at side \\(qR_0s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Require \\(n_j\\ge sP_0\\) for physical cutoff initialization.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  H_n(h)=n^{-2}\\sum_{v\\in(\\mathbb Z/n\\mathbb Z)^2}\n  h_v\\cos(2\\pi v_1/n),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  X_{n,s}=\\langle\\eta,f_{n,s}\\rangle,\\qquad\n  f_{n,s}(Q)=(s/n)^2\\cos(2\\pi x_{Q,1}/n),\n  \\]\n  where \\(x_Q\\) is the arithmetic center of the primary sites in \\(Q\\), and grid origins agree.</p><p class=\"li\">Both real tests annihilate constants. Let \\(\\bar\\eta\\) be the mean of cell observations, interpreted modulo \\(2\\pi\\), and use phase \\(e^{i\\bar\\eta}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  g=\\frac1{8\\pi^2},\\qquad\n  v_{n,s}^0=\n  \\langle f_{n,s},(I+aB_sA^+B_s^*)f_{n,s}\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Hats denote normalized cutoff expectations or variances.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On a fixed small complex ball of terminal activities, the cutoff variance correction and phase expectation are analytic. If\n\\[\n\\nu_j=|t_j|+|z_j|+\\|\\mathcal R^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{j|j}\\|,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{Var}}(X_{n_j,s})-v_{n_j,s}^0\n=\\kappa_{n_j,s}t_j+\nO(\\|\\mathcal R^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{j|j}\\|+\\nu_j^2),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\mathbb E}e^{i\\bar\\eta}\n=p_{j,s}z_j+\nO(\\|\\mathcal R^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{j|j}\\|+\\nu_j^2).\n\\]\nAll fixed activity derivatives have the corresponding Taylor bounds, uniformly in entry and stop. The multipliers are positive, bounded above and below, and\n\\[\nv_{n,s}^0=ag+O(s/n+n^{-2}),\\qquad\n\\kappa_{n,s}=ag+O(s/n+n^{-2}).\n\\]\nAt a common absolute stop, \\(p_{j,s}\\) for different entries agree except for their mean-observation-noise factor; their relative difference is\n\\(O((s_1/n)^2+(s_2/n)^2)\\).</p><p>The cutoff and observation-grid comparisons, including first temperature derivatives and divided differences, are those in Lemmas \\(4.1\\)–\\(4.2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The terminal analytic functionals are [15, (97)–(98), (116)–(119), (130)]. On the fixed \\(D_0^2\\)-block terminal grid, the Gaussian and common-mean integral of the subset sum is\n\\(1+O(D_0^2\\nu_j)\\), uniformly on a fixed complex source disk. Choose the activity ball so the error is below \\(1/2\\); normalized numerator and denominator are analytic and the denominator is nonzero.</p><p>Complete the Gaussian source \\(wX_{n,s}\\) before entry averaging. This gives\n\\(e^{v_{n,s}^0w^2/2}\\) and translates the interaction by\n\\[\nF(w)=w\\gamma_{n,s}\\cos(2\\pi v_1/n),\n\\qquad\n\\gamma_{n,s}=\\frac{2v_{n,s}^0}{\\alpha\\beta_{n,s}},\n\\qquad\n\\beta_{n,s}=1+O((s/n)^2).\n\\]\nThe gradient singleton contributes \\(t_j\\langle F,AF\\rangle/2\\) to the logarithm; mean integration removes the first harmonic at linear order. Since\n\\[\n\\langle\\cos(2\\pi v_1/n),A\\cos(2\\pi v_1/n)\\rangle\n=2n^2\\sin^2(\\pi/n),\n\\]\nthis gives the variance formula and multiplier asymptotics. The remainder costs its norm, and higher terms cost \\(O(\\nu_j^2)\\).</p><p>For the phase insertion, common-mean integration selects the fundamental singleton, with\n\\[\np_{j,s}=\\frac{D_0^2}{2}\n\\exp\\{\\tau_{j,s}/2-aD_{\\mathrm{tail},j}/2\\}&gt;0.\n\\]\nHere \\(D_{\\mathrm{tail},j}\\) is the diagonal retained mean-free covariance and \\(\\tau_{j,s}\\) is the integrated high-mean variance in height units, including observation noise. Both are bounded. At a common absolute stop, high covariances telescope to the same constant-mode continuation; only the noise variance \\((s/n)^2\\) depends on entry. Cauchy estimates give all fixed activity derivatives.</p><p>Lemmas \\(4.1\\)–\\(4.2\\) give the stated passage between cutoff readouts, different observation grids, and the same physical torus observables.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Replacing the original torus expectation by its normalized cutoff expectation changes the variance of \\(X_{n,s}\\) and the phase expectation by at most \\( Cn^Ce^{-cs^2}. \\) The same bound holds for their first …", "statement": "<p>**Cutoff error and its derivative — original Lemma 3.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b\\) be real in the initialization interval of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\ge sP_0\\) and \\(\\log n=o(s^2)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Replacing the original torus expectation by its normalized cutoff expectation changes the variance of \\(X_{n,s}\\) and the phase expectation by at most\n\\[\nCn^Ce^{-cs^2}.\n\\]\nThe same bound holds for their first \\(b\\)-derivatives and divided differences between any two real parameters in that interval.</p>", "proof": "<p>The cell-flow proof of the large-field reserve also gives probability at most \\(Ce^{-cs^2}\\) that a prescribed face difference exceeds \\(s/2\\), uniformly on the compact temperature interval. On its complement, \\(1-\\chi_s\\) has that bound. Sum over faces and apply Hölder with the reference moments of Proposition \\(1\\), as in [15, Lemma 4.4]. This controls unnormalized loss for the phase and first two moments of \\(X_{n,s}\\). The normalized cutoff mass is \\(1-o(1)\\).</p><p>For derivatives, insert the Poisson score \\(N/b\\). Lemma \\(1.3\\) bounds fixed moments of \\(N\\) by a fixed power of \\(n\\). Hölder applied to the score, observable, and cutoff loss retains \\(e^{-c's^2}\\), while other factors cost powers of \\(n\\). The derivative of the cutoff normalizer has the same bound because it stays away from zero. Integrating the derivative estimate gives divided-difference control.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After removing its deterministic mean-noise factor, the phase expectation is exactly \\(\\mathbb Ee^{i\\bar h}\\), independently of \\(s\\).", "statement": "<p>**Observation-grid comparison — original Lemma 3.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use compatible observation grids on a torus of side \\(n\\), with \\(n/s\\) sufficiently large, and let\n\\[\nB_s\\cos(2\\pi v_1/n)\n=\\beta_{n,s}\\cos(2\\pi x_{Q,1}/n).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After removing its deterministic mean-noise factor, the phase expectation is exactly\n\\(\\mathbb Ee^{i\\bar h}\\), independently of \\(s\\). After subtracting independent observation-noise variance and dividing by \\(\\beta_{n,s}^2\\), the variance of \\(X_{n,s}\\) differs from \\(\\operatorname{Var}H_n\\) by \\(O((s/n)^4)\\). Their first \\(b\\)-derivatives and divided differences over a compact real temperature interval differ by at most\n\\[\nCn^2(s/n)^4.\n\\]\nCutoff observables incur the additional error of Lemma \\(4.1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The mean of \\(B_sh\\) equals the microscopic mean. Independent mean noise has variance \\((s/n)^2\\), proving the phase identity.</p><p>The profile \\(B_s^*f_{n,s}\\) projects onto the microscopic first cosine with coefficient \\(\\beta_{n,s}\\), with no first sine component by reflection about arithmetic cell centers. Remaining Fourier modes have frequency-index distance at least \\(cn/s\\) from zero. Their total squared coefficient norm is at most\n\\(Cn^{-2}(s/n)^2\\), while \\(A^+\\) restricted to these modes has norm at most \\(Cs^2\\). Their reference cost is \\(O((s/n)^4)\\).</p><p>Translation invariance makes distinct Fourier modes exactly orthogonal. Thus the variance is the first-cosine variance plus alias variance, without a cross term. Lemma \\(1.2\\) controls the alias variance. Lemma \\(1.5\\) controls its derivative by reference cost times \\(|E|=2n^2\\). The factor \\(\\beta_{n,s}^{-2}\\) is bounded and independent of \\(b\\). Integration gives divided differences.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fixed parameters and entry threshold can be chosen to give critical histories \\(\\mathcal H^{(k)}\\) at \\(b=b_c\\), with admissible \\(s_k\\asymp2^k\\) and entry indices \\(j_0(k)=O(k)\\), such that for each fixed \\(0<c<c'<3\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Critical histories — original Proposition 3.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Propositions \\(2\\)–\\(4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A history is the plane sequence initialized wholly in its remainder, together with separately initialized tori at valid stops.</p><p class=\"li\">The index \\(j\\) is always absolute.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Fixed parameters and entry threshold can be chosen to give critical histories \\(\\mathcal H^{(k)}\\) at \\(b=b_c\\), with admissible \\(s_k\\asymp2^k\\) and entry indices \\(j_0(k)=O(k)\\), such that for each fixed \\(0&lt;c&lt;c'&lt;3\\), uniformly on \\(c2^k\\le j\\le c'2^k\\),\n\\[\nW_j^{(k)}\n=\\frac{(1,1)}j+O\\!\\left(\\frac{\\log(2+j)}{j^2}\\right),\n\\qquad\n\\|Q_j^{(k)}\\|+\\|\\mathcal R_j^{(k)}\\|=O(j^{-2}).\n\\]\nSeparately initialized torus remainders are \\(O(j^{-2})\\) on these stage ranges behind every valid later stop in the same history up to \\(4\\cdot2^k\\).</p><p>More generally, for any prescribed sufficiently small tube and any integer bound \\(J_{\\max}(s)=o(s^2)\\), the critical history at admissible side \\(s\\) remains in that tube through \\(J_{\\max}(s)\\), for all large \\(s\\). Every stage of its separately initialized tori with valid stops below \\(J_{\\max}(s)\\) remains in the corresponding small ball. Geometric choices are independent of \\(s,J_{\\max}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The family and confinement through \\(4\\cdot2^k\\) are [15, Proposition 5.1 and Lemma 5.5]. For \\(Y_j=|Z_j|\\), [15, (135)–(137)] give\n\\[\nu_j\\asymp Y_j\\asymp j^{-1},\\qquad\nu_j=\\sqrt{H/b_*}\\,Y_j+O(j^{-2}),\n\\]\n\\[\nu_j=(b_*j)^{-1}+O(\\log(2+j)/j^2).\n\\]\nThe overlap bounds [15, Lemma 5.7] transfer both coordinates with \\(O(j^{-2})\\) error. The phase formula [15, (130)], positivity of physical characteristic coefficients, and exponentially small cutoff loss select \\(Z_j&gt;0\\). Rescale as in Proposition \\(3\\). The plane remainder estimate is part of the same critical proposition and odd-cone estimate.</p><p>For a larger stopped torus, separate contraction is driven by copied scalar coordinates. Contracting from an earlier fractional range suppresses its initial contribution exponentially and sums an \\(O(j^{-2})\\) forcing, giving all intermediate-stage torus bounds.</p><p>For extended confinement, [15, Lemma 5.4] supplies the critical first-cosine Gaussian limit and factorization with the mean phase. For any fixed tolerance, first \\(P_0\\), then \\(s\\), can be chosen so that variance and mixed-phase discrepancies are below tolerance for every compatible \\(n\\ge sP_0\\). No convergence rate in \\(n\\) is needed. The mixed-phase multiplier on \\(z\\), and the variance multiplier on \\(t\\), are nonzero.</p><p>Suppose there is a first exit from a tube of radius \\(\\rho\\). Before exit, plane and torus contractions give\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal R_j\\|+\\|\\mathcal R^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{j|j}\\|\n\\le C\\theta^{j-j_0}\\rho_{\\mathrm{ent}}+C\\rho^2.\n\\]\nAt the candidate exit, the bounded map leaves states in a fixed multiple of the tube, so terminal readouts still apply. The variance and mixed-phase identities bound singleton coefficients by\n\\[\nC(\\rho_{\\mathrm{ent}}+\\rho^2+\\delta_{\\mathrm{phys}}+\\delta_{\\mathrm{cut}}).\n\\]\nOnly a fixed number,\n\\[\n\\max\\!\\left(0,\\left\\lceil\\log_L\\frac{R_0^4}{D_0q}\\right\\rceil\\right),\n\\]\nof initial indices have \\(n_j&lt;sP_0\\); absorb their amplification into the entry smallness. Uniformly for \\(j\\le J_{\\max}(s)\\),\n\\[\n\\delta_{\\mathrm{cut}}\n\\le Cn_j^Ce^{-cs^2}\n\\le\\exp\\{O(J_{\\max}(s))-cs^2\\}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\rho\\) small, then entry and physical discrepancies small relative to \\(\\rho\\), then \\(s\\) large. The bound excludes first exit. Separate torus contractions give confinement at all earlier stages.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exists \\(\\mathcal P\\in(0,\\infty)\\) such that \\( \\sup_{\\frac12\\,2^k\\le j\\le\\frac52\\,2^k} \\left|\\frac{V_j^{(k)}}j-\\mathcal Pq_u\\right| \\longrightarrow0. \\) At such stages, the plane transverse remainder and every …", "statement": "<p>**Nonzero temperature response — original Proposition 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the histories \\(\\mathcal H^{(k)}\\) of Proposition \\(5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Enlarge the remainder contraction slightly to \\(\\theta_1&lt;1\\), choose \\(r\\in(\\theta_1,1)\\), and choose \\(C_*\\) so large that\n  \\[\n  C_*\\log L&gt;1000,\\qquad\n  \\frac2{C_*}&lt;\\frac{-\\log r}{100}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\gamma C_*&lt;1/100,\\qquad\n  \\gamma&lt;-\\log r/100.\n  \\]\n  Choose the activity tube so the plane derivative norm in balanced coordinates is at most \\(e^\\gamma\\), while remainder contraction stays at most \\(\\theta_1\\). Fix entry smallness and thresholds afterward.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  V_j^{(k)}\n  =\\left.\\partial_bW_j^{(k)}(b)\\right|_{b=b_c},\n  \\qquad q_u=(1,-1/2).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists \\(\\mathcal P\\in(0,\\infty)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\frac12\\,2^k\\le j\\le\\frac52\\,2^k}\n\\left|\\frac{V_j^{(k)}}j-\\mathcal Pq_u\\right|\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nAt such stages, the plane transverse remainder and every separately initialized torus remainder behind a valid stop in that history have temperature derivative bounded by\n\\[\nC|V_j^{(k)}|/j+e^{-cj}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Entry analyticity gives initial derivative bound \\(Cs_k^2\\). Propagation in the tube gives \\(Cs_k^2e^{\\gamma j}\\) for plane derivatives and the weaker common bound \\(Cs_k^2e^{2\\gamma j}\\) including torus derivatives. These bounds only remove initial remainder contributions.</p><p>On a slightly larger fractional interval, Proposition \\(5\\) gives\n\\[\nW_j=\\frac{(1,1)}j+O(\\log j/j^2),\n\\qquad\n\\|Q_j\\|+\\|\\mathcal R^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{j|h}\\|=O(j^{-2}).\n\\]\nDifferentiating Proposition \\(3\\),\n\\[\nV_{j+1}=A_jV_j+B_j\\partial_bQ_j,\n\\qquad\nA_j=I+\\frac Mj+O(\\log j/j^2),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|B_j\\|\\le C/j,\n\\qquad\nM=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-2\\\\-1&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nFor fixed torus stop \\(h\\), set\n\\[\nr_{j|h}=\\|\\partial_bQ_j\\|+\n\\|\\partial_b\\mathcal R^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{j|h}\\|.\n\\]\nAnalytic remainder estimates and absence of linear singleton forcing give\n\\[\nr_{j+1|h}\\le\\theta_1r_{j|h}+\\frac Cj|V_j|.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(j\\), let \\(l=\\lfloor j/2\\rfloor\\), and define\n\\[\nS_v=\\max_{l\\le i\\le j}r^{j-i}|V_i|,\n\\qquad\nS_q=\\max_{l\\le i\\le j}r^{j-i}r_{i|h}.\n\\]\nContraction yields\n\\[\nS_q\\le \\frac CjS_v+Cr^{j-l}r_{l|h}.\n\\]\nFor large \\(j\\), \\(A_i\\) is invertible and\n\\(\\|A_i^{-1}\\|\\le1+C/j\\). Solve the scalar recurrence backward, weight by \\(r^{j-i}\\), and sum geometric factors:\n\\[\nS_v\\le C|V_j|+\\frac CjS_q.\n\\]\nAbsorb the \\(j^{-2}S_q\\) term. The crude initial derivative bound and choice of \\(\\gamma\\) make the homogeneous term exponentially small. Thus\n\\[\nr_{j|h}\\le \\frac Cj|V_j|+e^{-cj}.\n\\]\nThe same argument gives the plane bound.</p><p>The height part of the critical first-cosine observation has variance bounded below and bounded reference cost. Lemma \\(1.5\\) gives a positive lower bound on its temperature derivative. Lemma \\(4.1\\) transfers this to the cutoff readout. Differentiating Proposition \\(4\\) and using the remainder estimate bounds it by \\(C|V_j|+e^{-cj}\\). Hence\n\\[\n|V_j|\\ge c&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Compare entries through the physical diagnostic\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_{j,s}(b)=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n(\\widehat{\\operatorname{Var}}_bX_{n_j,s}-\\|f_{n_j,s}\\|_2^2)/\\beta_{n_j,s}^2\\\\\ne^{(s/n_j)^2/2}\\widehat{\\mathbb E}_be^{i\\bar\\eta}\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nLemma \\(4.2\\) compares it to the same physical pair\n\\((\\operatorname{Var}^{\\mathrm{tor}}_bH_{n_j},\n\\mathbb E_b^{\\mathrm{tor}}e^{i\\bar h})\\).\nAt zero activity its scalar linear coefficient in \\(W,Q\\) coordinates is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diag}\\!\\left(\n\\frac{\\kappa_{n_j,s}}{b_*\\beta_{n_j,s}^2},\n\\frac{e^{(s/n_j)^2/2}p_{j,s}\\ell_j^{-1}}{\\sqrt{b_*H}}\n\\right).\n\\]\nIts background is independent of \\(b\\). Differentiation and the remainder estimate give\n\\[\nD_jV_j+O(|V_j|/j)+O(e^{-cj}),\n\\qquad\nD_j=\\operatorname{diag}\\!\\left(\\frac{ag}{b_*},\n\\frac{p_j}{\\sqrt{b_*H}}\\right).\n\\]\nThe same uniformly invertible \\(D_j\\) can be used at a common stop for both entries; multiplier differences are exponentially small. Lemmas \\(4.1\\)–\\(4.2\\) give exponentially small physical comparison errors when \\(s\\asymp j\\). Therefore\n\\[\n|V_j^{(k)}-V_j^{(k')}|\n\\le\\frac Cj\\bigl(|V_j^{(k)}|+|V_j^{(k')}|\\bigr)\n\\]\non common interior ranges. No remainder spaces are subtracted.</p><p>Splice \\(V_j=V_j^{(k)}\\) for \\(2^k\\le j&lt;2^{k+1}\\). At ordinary steps,\n\\[\nV_{j+1}=(I+M/j+E_j)V_j,\n\\qquad \\|E_j\\|\\le C\\log j/j^2.\n\\]\nNonvanishing permits exponentially small error vectors to be represented as matrix errors. Entry changes contribute an additional \\(C/j\\) error.</p><p>The eigenvectors of \\(M\\) are\n\\(q_u=(1,-1/2)\\), \\(q_s=(1,1)\\), with eigenvalues \\(1,-2\\). If \\((U_k,S_k)\\) are coordinates of \\(V_{2^k}\\), dyadic products give\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}U_{k+1}\\\\S_{k+1}\\end{pmatrix}\n=\n\\left[\n\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;0\\\\0&amp;1/4\\end{pmatrix}+E_k\n\\right]\n\\begin{pmatrix}U_k\\\\S_k\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\|E_k\\|\\le Ck2^{-k}.\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\(\\prod_{j=n}^{2n-1}(1+1/j)=2\\) and\n\\(\\prod_{j=n}^{2n-1}(1-2/j)=1/4+O(n^{-1})\\);\npartial products and inverses on dyadic intervals are bounded, and the error sums are \\(O(k2^{-k})\\).</p><p>For large \\(k\\), the cone \\(|S_k|\\le|U_k|\\) is invariant. A sequence never entering it would satisfy\n\\(|S_{k+1}|\\le\\frac12|S_k|\\), so both coordinates would tend to zero, contrary to \\(|V_j|\\ge c\\). Once in the cone,\n\\[\nU_{k+1}=2U_k(1+\\eta_k),\n\\qquad |\\eta_k|\\le Ck2^{-k}.\n\\]\nTail factors are positive and their logarithms summable. Thus \\(U_k/2^k\\) has a finite nonzero limit \\(\\mathcal P\\). Also\n\\[\n\\frac{|S_{k+1}|}{|U_{k+1}|}\n\\le\\rho\\frac{|S_k|}{|U_k|}+Ck2^{-k},\n\\qquad\\rho&lt;1,\n\\]\nso the ratio tends to zero. Partial dyadic products and entry comparison give the asserted uniform limit.</p><p>Finally, the first component of the physical readout has positive temperature response. If \\(\\mathcal P&lt;0\\), its leading term would be negative of order \\(j\\). Therefore \\(\\mathcal P&gt;0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Uniformly for \\(w=\\delta j\\) in compact subintervals of \\((0,T)\\), \\( \\frac{x_j(b)}\\delta\\longrightarrow k_0\\cot(k_0w), \\qquad \\frac{y_j(b)}\\delta\\longrightarrow k_0\\csc(k_0w), \\qquad \\|Q_j(b)\\|=O(\\delta^2). \\) For …", "statement": "<p>**Passage time and variance diagnostic — original Proposition 4.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed choices of Proposition \\(6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\varepsilon=b_c-b&gt;0,\\qquad\n  \\delta=\\sqrt\\varepsilon,\\qquad\n  s\\asymp\\varepsilon^{-1/3},\\qquad\n  J=\\lfloor C_*\\log s\\rfloor,\n  \\]\n  with admissible \\(s\\). Thus\n  \\[\n  \\varepsilon s^2\\to0,\\qquad\n  \\delta^{-1}=o(s^2),\\qquad J=o(\\delta^{-1}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  k_0=\\sqrt{3\\mathcal P},\\qquad T=\\pi/k_0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Torus laws are original periodic Bessel height laws in the zero-winding sector, with common translations factored out.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly for \\(w=\\delta j\\) in compact subintervals of \\((0,T)\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{x_j(b)}\\delta\\longrightarrow k_0\\cot(k_0w),\n\\qquad\n\\frac{y_j(b)}\\delta\\longrightarrow k_0\\csc(k_0w),\n\\qquad\n\\|Q_j(b)\\|=O(\\delta^2).\n\\]\nFor every sequence \\(\\delta j\\to w\\in(0,T)\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{b,n_j}H_{n_j}\\longrightarrow ag.\n\\]</p><p>For every sufficiently small fixed \\(d&gt;0\\), there are exit indices \\(j_e=j_e(\\varepsilon,d)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\delta j_e\\to T,\\qquad\nx_{j_e}=-d+O(d^2),\\qquad y_{j_e}=O(d),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|Q_{j_e}\\|+\\|\\mathcal R^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{j_e|j_e}\\|=O(d^2).\n\\]\nAll states through exit lie in the small-activity domain. For some \\(c_d&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\limsup_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\n\\operatorname{Var}^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{b,n_{j_e}}H_{n_{j_e}}\n\\le ag-c_d.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(\\Delta W_i=W_i(b_c-\\varepsilon)-W_i(b_c)\\), and similarly \\(\\Delta Q_i\\). For sufficiently small fixed \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), first prove uniformly on \\(J\\le i\\le\\eta/\\delta\\),\n\\[\n\\Delta W_i=-\\varepsilon i(\\mathcal Pq_u+O(\\eta^2)+o(1)),\n\\qquad \\|\\Delta Q_i\\|\\le C\\varepsilon.\n\\]</p><p>Bootstrap \\(|\\Delta W_i|\\le C_1\\varepsilon i\\). Lemma \\(7.1\\) applies through fixed multiples of \\(\\delta^{-1}\\). Differentiated remainder bounds give\n\\[\n\\|\\Delta Q_{i+1}\\|\n\\le\\theta_1\\|\\Delta Q_i\\|\n+C_{C_1}(i^{-1}\\varepsilon i+(\\varepsilon i)^2).\n\\]\nThe forcing is \\(O_{C_1}(\\varepsilon)\\); Lemma \\(7.2\\) and contraction give\n\\(\\|\\Delta Q_i\\|\\le C_{C_1}\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>Subtract scalar recurrences and linearize their quadratic terms about the critical trajectory:\n\\[\n\\Delta W_{i+1}=(I+M/i)\\Delta W_i+e_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n|e_i|\\le C_{C_1}\n\\left(\\frac{\\varepsilon\\log i}{i}\n+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{i}+\\varepsilon^2i^2\\right).\n\\]\nThese errors respectively use the critical correction and cubic derivatives, the \\(O(i^{-1})\\) remainder row, and the quadratic perturbation.</p><p>In the eigenbasis of \\(M\\), products from \\(h\\) to \\(i\\) have norm at most \\(Ci/h\\), and send \\(q_u\\) exactly to \\((i/h)q_u\\). Variation of constants, divided by \\(\\varepsilon i\\), bounds total error by\n\\[\no(1)+C_{C_1}\\sum_{h\\ge J}\\frac{\\log h+1}{h^2}\n+C_{C_1}\\varepsilon\\sum_{h\\le i}h\n\\le o(1)+C_{C_1}(\\log J/J+\\eta^2).\n\\]\nChoose \\(C_1\\) larger than the leading bound, then \\(\\eta\\) small, closing the bootstrap. The same contraction controls each stopped torus remainder.</p><p>Interpolate \\(x_j/\\delta,y_j/\\delta\\) at times \\(w=\\delta j\\). On compact subintervals of \\((0,\\eta]\\), these coordinates are bounded, \\(Q_j=O(\\delta^2)\\), and map errors are \\(O(\\delta^3)\\). Slopes are bounded and every subsequential limit solves\n\\[\nX'=-Y^2,\\qquad Y'=-XY.\n\\]\nThe perturbation estimate with arbitrarily small \\(\\eta\\) gives\n\\[\n(X(w),Y(w))\n=\\frac{(1,1)}w-w(\\mathcal Pq_u+O(w^2))\n\\quad(w\\downarrow0).\n\\]\nThe invariant \\(X^2-Y^2\\) equals \\(-3\\mathcal P\\). Thus\n\\(X'=-(X^2+3\\mathcal P)\\). Its pole at zero and positive \\(Y\\) uniquely give\n\\[\nX(w)=k_0\\cot(k_0w),\\qquad\nY(w)=k_0\\csc(k_0w).\n\\]\nAll subsequential limits agree.</p><p>Extend to any compact subinterval of \\((0,T)\\). Starting at fixed positive time, take a bound larger than the limiting solution there. While discrete scaled coordinates remain bounded, contraction gives \\(Q_j=O(\\delta^2)\\) and vanishing Euler error. The quadratic vector field is Lipschitz on this bounded set. Discrete Gronwall gives convergence and excludes first exit from the larger bound.</p><p>For the final outgoing segment, fix \\(w_0\\in(T/2,T)\\), and let \\(j_e\\) be the first index after \\(\\lfloor w_0/\\delta\\rfloor\\) with \\(-x_{j_e}\\ge d\\). Take fixed large \\(M&gt;k_0\\), and outgoing time \\(w_M\\) where \\(Y(w_M)=M\\). Compact convergence gives\n\\[\ny_j=(M+o(1))\\delta,\\qquad\n-x_j/y_j=1+O(M^{-2})+o(1)\n\\]\nat \\(\\delta j\\to w_M\\), together with\n\\[\n\\|Q_j\\|+\\|\\mathcal R^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{j|h}\\|\n\\le KM^2\\delta^2\n\\]\nwith \\(K\\) independent of large fixed \\(M\\). To obtain this independence, use the fixed scaled interval where outgoing \\(Y\\) grows from \\(M/2\\) to \\(M\\); there are arbitrarily many discrete steps, scalar size is at most \\(CM\\delta\\), and contraction removes the earlier remainder.</p><p>For small coordinates consider\n\\[\ny&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\frac12\\le q:=-x/y\\le2,\\qquad\n\\|Q\\|+\\|\\mathcal R^{\\mathrm{tor}}\\|\\le K_1y^2.\n\\]\nThe map gives\n\\[\ny_+=y\\{1+qy+O_{K_1}(y^2+j^{-2}y)\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nq_+-q=y(1-q^2)+O_{K_1}(y^2+j^{-2}y).\n\\]\nAt the ratio endpoints the leading term points inward; a sufficiently small step cannot cross the remaining interior margin. Hence the ratio interval is invariant. Also\n\\[\ny+cy^2\\le y_+\\le y+Cy^2.\n\\]\nChoose \\(K_1\\) large enough for remainder contraction to preserve its condition, using \\(y_+\\ge y\\).</p><p>For large fixed \\(M\\), the trajectory enters this cone. Choose \\(d\\) small so it applies until and including the first crossing of \\(-x=d\\). Before crossing, \\(y\\le2d\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac1{y_i}-\\frac1{y_{i+1}}\\ge c'&gt;0.\n\\]\nThus exit occurs within \\(C/(M\\delta)\\) additional steps. Its overshoot is \\(O(y^2)=O(d^2)\\), proving all unscaled exit bounds.</p><p>Compact convergence before \\(T\\) gives \\(\\liminf\\delta j_e\\ge T\\), while\n\\[\n\\limsup_{\\delta\\downarrow0}\\delta j_e\\le w_M+C/M.\n\\]\nLet \\(M\\to\\infty\\), so \\(w_M\\uparrow T\\). All coordinates remain in the small map domain; no map is used beyond it.</p><p>At interior stops, Proposition \\(4\\) gives variance tending to \\(ag\\). At exit,\n\\[\nt_{j_e}=-d/b_*+O(d^2),\n\\qquad\n\\|\\mathcal R^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{j_e|j_e}\\|+\\nu_{j_e}^2=O(d^2).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{b,n_{j_e}}H_{n_{j_e}}\n=ag-\\frac{ag}{b_*}d+O(d^2)+o_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}(1).\n\\]\nLemmas \\(4.1\\)–\\(4.2\\) remove cutoff, noise, and sampling errors, since\n\\(\\log n_j=O(\\delta^{-1})=o(s^2)\\) and \\(s/n_j\\to0\\). For small \\(d\\), take for example \\(c_d=agd/(2b_*)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniformly for \\(J/2\\le i\\le K(s)\\), \\( W_i=\\frac{(1,1)}i+O(\\log i/i^2), \\qquad \\|Q_i\\|+\\|\\mathcal R_i\\|=O(i^{-2}). \\) Every separately initialized torus with valid stop \\(h\\in[i,K(s)]\\) also satisfies …", "statement": "<p>**Critical estimates after a logarithmic initial interval — original Lemma 4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(s\\to\\infty\\) through admissible sides, \\(J=\\lfloor C_*\\log s\\rfloor\\), and\n\\(K(s)\\ge J\\) with \\(K(s)=o(s^2)\\). Consider the history at side \\(s\\), at \\(b=b_c\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly for \\(J/2\\le i\\le K(s)\\),\n\\[\nW_i=\\frac{(1,1)}i+O(\\log i/i^2),\n\\qquad\n\\|Q_i\\|+\\|\\mathcal R_i\\|=O(i^{-2}).\n\\]\nEvery separately initialized torus with valid stop \\(h\\in[i,K(s)]\\) also satisfies\n\\(\\|\\mathcal R^{\\mathrm{tor}}_{i|h}\\|=O(i^{-2})\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition \\(5\\) confines all states to the chosen tube. Put \\(i_0=\\lceil J/100\\rceil\\). For \\(i\\ge i_0\\), compare with a canonical history \\(\\mathcal H^{(k)}\\) having \\(2^k\\le i&lt;2^{k+1}\\). Both describe the same physical torus \\(n_i=D_0L^i\\). The choice \\(C_*\\log L&gt;1000\\) makes \\(s/n_i\\le e^{-ci}\\), so sampling and multiplier errors are exponentially small. Cutoff errors are bounded by\n\\[\nC\\exp\\{CK(s)-cs^2\\},\n\\]\nalso exponentially small in \\(i\\), since \\(K(s)=o(s^2)\\).</p><p>Let \\(d_i=|t_i|+|z_i|\\), and let \\(R_i\\) be the maximum plane remainder norm and stage-\\(i\\) torus remainder norms for stops in \\([i,K(s)]\\). Nonzero readout multipliers give\n\\[\nd_i\\le C/i+CR_i+Cd_i^2.\n\\]\nRemainder contraction with copied scalar forcing gives\n\\[\nR_{i+1}\\le\\theta_1R_i+Cd_i^2.\n\\]\nThe torus family in the maximum decreases with \\(i\\), so the inequality remains valid.</p><p>Absorb \\(Cd_i^2\\). Then \\(d_i\\le C/i+CR_i\\); smallness of \\(R_i\\) gives\n\\[\nR_{i+1}\\le\\theta_2R_i+C/i^2,\\qquad\\theta_2&lt;1.\n\\]\nIteration yields \\(R_i\\le C\\theta_2^{i-i_0}+C/i^2\\), and for \\(i\\ge J/2\\) the first term is \\(O(i^{-2})\\). Thus \\(R_i=O(i^{-2})\\), \\(d_i=O(i^{-1})\\).</p><p>Repeat the readout comparison. All nonlinear and remainder errors are now \\(O(i^{-2})\\), so unbalanced scalar coordinates agree with canonical ones to this order. The normal-coordinate changes differ from their linear parts by \\(O(i^{-2})\\). Proposition \\(5\\) therefore gives the asserted critical estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\frac{W_J(b_c-\\varepsilon)-W_J(b_c)}{-\\varepsilon} =J(\\mathcal Pq_u+o(1)), \\) \\( \\left\\|\\frac{Q_J(b_c-\\varepsilon)-Q_J(b_c)}{\\varepsilon}\\right\\|\\le C. \\) The same uniform bound holds for each unbalanced torus …", "statement": "<p>**Matching the temperature secant — original Lemma 4.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(\\varepsilon,\\delta,s,J\\) from Proposition \\(7\\), with the history initialized at side \\(s\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac{W_J(b_c-\\varepsilon)-W_J(b_c)}{-\\varepsilon}\n=J(\\mathcal Pq_u+o(1)),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\frac{Q_J(b_c-\\varepsilon)-Q_J(b_c)}{\\varepsilon}\\right\\|\\le C.\n\\]\nThe same uniform bound holds for each unbalanced torus remainder secant at stage \\(J\\), behind a stop of order \\(\\delta^{-1}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Entry analyticity and propagation give, through \\(J\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{distance}(\\text{states at }b_c-\\varepsilon,b_c)\n\\le C\\varepsilon s^2e^{2\\gamma J}.\n\\]\nEach history uses its own norm; torus bounds follow by contraction with the same scalars. Since \\(\\varepsilon\\asymp s^{-3}\\) and \\(2\\gamma C_*&lt;1\\), the bound decays as a negative power of \\(s\\), hence faster than any fixed negative power of \\(J\\). A fixed margin to the tube boundary justifies propagation.</p><p>Let \\(\\widetilde V_i\\) be scalar secants with denominator \\(-\\varepsilon\\). On \\([J/2,J]\\), Lemma \\(7.1\\) and the preceding closeness permit integration of analytic derivatives between the two states. The scalar and remainder recurrences have the same form as in Proposition \\(6\\); extra coefficients are exponentially small in \\(J\\). Crude remainder secants are at most \\(Cs^2e^{2\\gamma J}\\). The weighted backward estimates of Proposition \\(6\\), on a fixed-fraction interval ending at \\(J\\), yield\n\\[\n\\widetilde r_J\\le C|\\widetilde V_J|/J+e^{-cJ}.\n\\]\nThe homogeneous term is bounded by \\(Cr^{c_1J}s^2e^{2\\gamma J}\\), exponentially small by the choices of \\(C_*,\\gamma\\). This is uniform behind later torus stops.</p><p>Choose a canonical history with \\(s_k\\asymp J\\) and \\(J\\) in its interior range. Its stopped states remain analytic and bounded on a parameter disk\n\\[\n\\rho_J=cJ^{-2}e^{-2\\gamma J}.\n\\]\nIndeed the entry displacement is at most \\(Cs_k^2\\rho_J\\), and its amplification is at most \\(Ce^{\\gamma J}\\), tending to zero; torus contraction gives the same control. Cauchy bounds second derivatives by \\(C\\rho_J^{-2}\\). Since\n\\(\\log\\varepsilon=-3J/C_*+O(1)\\),\n\\[\n\\varepsilon\\rho_J^{-2}\n\\le CJ^4e^{(-3/C_*+4\\gamma)J}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nHence its scalar secant is \\(V_J^{(k)}+o(1)\\), with analogous absolute remainder control.</p><p>Compare physical readout secants using Lemmas \\(4.1\\)–\\(4.2\\), whose derivative bounds apply to divided differences. Taylor expansion along each history's temperature segment gives\n\\[\nD_J\\widetilde V_J\n=D_JV_J^{(k)}\n+O\\!\\left(\\frac{|\\widetilde V_J|+|V_J^{(k)}|}{J}\\right)+o(1).\n\\]\nThe same uniformly invertible \\(D_J\\) is used. Sampling and multiplier errors are exponentially small; in particular \\(n_J^2(s/n_J)^4\\le e^{-cJ}\\).</p><p>Proposition \\(6\\) gives \\(|V_J^{(k)}|=O(J)\\). Absorption yields \\(|\\widetilde V_J|=O(J)\\), then\n\\(\\widetilde V_J-V_J^{(k)}=O(1)+o(1)\\). Proposition \\(6\\) and the remainder-secant estimate give the conclusions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every such temperature sequence has a subsequence and a nonincreasing, right-continuous function \\( \\mathcal A:(0,\\infty)\\to[0,a],\\qquad a=8\\pi, \\) such that, at every continuity point \\(w>0\\), every integer sequence …", "statement": "<p>**Extraction on logarithmic scales — original Proposition 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b_k&lt;b_c\\), \\(b_k\\to b_c\\), and \\(\\delta_k=\\sqrt{b_c-b_k}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All temperatures lie in a fixed compact subinterval of \\((0,\\infty)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B_m=\\{0,\\ldots,m-1\\}^2\\), with free unit-edge Laplacian \\(A_{B_m}\\), and define\n  \\[\n  g_m(x)=x_1/m,\\qquad\n  a_m(b)=\\operatorname{Var}^{\\mathrm{free}}_{B_m,b}(h,A_{B_m}g_m).\n  \\]\n  This test is the difference of right and left face averages. The two coordinate affine tests have covariance matrix \\(a_m(b)I_2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The reference bounds imply \\(a_m(b)\\le C\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every such temperature sequence has a subsequence and a nonincreasing, right-continuous function\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:(0,\\infty)\\to[0,a],\\qquad a=8\\pi,\n\\]\nsuch that, at every continuity point \\(w&gt;0\\), every integer sequence satisfying\n\\(\\delta_k\\log_Lm_k\\to w\\) obeys\n\\[\na_{m_k}(b_k)\\longrightarrow\\mathcal A(w).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Cutting an \\(M\\)-square into \\(m\\)-squares and strips of width below \\(m\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sqrt{a_M(b)}\n\\le\\sqrt{a_m(b)}+C(m^{-1}+m/M)^{1/2},\n\\qquad M\\ge m.\n\\]\nIndeed, omit seam and strip flows from the large affine test. Their squared reference cost is \\(O(m^{-1}+m/M)\\). Releasing cell constraints increases covariance and makes retained cells independent. The \\(L^2\\) triangle inequality and Proposition \\(1\\) give the bound, uniformly in temperature; this is [14, (2.6)].</p><p>For positive rational \\(q\\), set \\(m_k(q)=\\lfloor L^{q/\\delta_k}\\rfloor\\). A diagonal subsequence gives limits \\(\\alpha(q)\\) of \\(a_{m_k(q)}(b_k)\\). For \\(q&lt;q'\\), the cutting inequality orders the limits. Define\n\\[\n\\mathcal A(w)=\\sup_{q&gt;w,\\ q\\in\\mathbb Q}\\alpha(q).\n\\]\nThis is nonincreasing and right-continuous. Rational jump values need not be preserved.</p><p>For \\(q_-&lt;w&lt;q_+\\), an arbitrary sequence at logarithmic scale \\(w\\) eventually lies between \\(m_k(q_-)\\) and \\(m_k(q_+)\\). Applying the cutting inequality in both directions bounds limsup and liminf of its square-root coefficient by the rational limits. Let \\(q_\\pm\\to w\\) at a continuity point.</p><p>Finally, covariance monotonicity gives \\(a_m(b_k)\\le a_m(b_c)\\). By [14, Theorem 2.4 and Section 6], \\(a_m(b_c)\\to a=8\\pi\\). Thus \\(0\\le\\mathcal A\\le a\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Free rectangular laws have the Gaussian Laplace, covariance, and moment limits of Lemma \\(9.1\\), including \\(a_*=0\\).", "statement": "<p>**Gaussian laws at continuity scales and zero-interval transforms — original Proposition 5.5 and Corollary 5.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use an extracted sequence and profile from Proposition \\(8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a continuity point \\(w&gt;0\\), a sequence \\(n_k\\) with\n  \\(\\delta_k\\log_Ln_k\\to w\\), and put \\(a_*=\\mathcal A(w)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Multiplication of \\(n_k\\) by a fixed positive factor, or bounded lattice rounding, leaves this logarithmic scale unchanged.</p><p class=\"li\">A lattice profile represents a continuum density if its piecewise constant scaled density converges in \\(L^2\\). For bounded piecewise continuous \\(f\\), one may use \\(n_k^{-2}f(x/n_k)\\), with cell quadrature along discontinuities. If \\(\\int f=0\\), adjust the discrete total exactly to zero by subtracting its total times uniform probability.</p><p class=\"li\">Smooth shifts are sampled as \\(g_k(x)=g(x/n_k)\\); face probabilities are normalized by their primary vertex counts.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li\">Free rectangular laws have the Gaussian Laplace, covariance, and moment limits of Lemma \\(9.1\\), including \\(a_*=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(a_*&gt;0\\), their fractional means converge independently to uniform measure as in Lemma \\(9.2\\), and their fixed primary pinned geometries have the Gaussian limits of Lemma \\(9.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In particular, an inner square surrounded by a fixed positive-width zero rim has covariance \\(a_*(-\\Delta_D)^{-1}\\) for bounded density smears; their totals need not vanish.</p><p class=\"li\">On the zero-winding torus of side \\(n_k\\), for every smooth periodic shift \\(g\\),\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Var}(h,A_{\\mathrm{tor}}g_k)\n  \\longrightarrow a_*\\int_{\\mathbb T^2}|\\nabla g|^2.\n  \\]\n  Hence the first-cosine observation \\(H_{n_k}\\) has limiting variance \\(a_*g\\), where \\(g=1/(8\\pi^2)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Uniform affine-transform convergence on compact zero-profile intervals is given by Lemma \\(9.4\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(9.1\\)–\\(9.3\\) give the free, phase, and pinned assertions. For a positive-width zero rim \\(p=U\\setminus D\\), \\(\\mathcal H_p\\) identifies with \\(H_0^1(D)\\) by zero extension, giving Dirichlet covariance.</p><p>For the torus upper bound, cut into small fixed rectangles, restrict the gradient test, and apply Lemma \\(9.1\\)'s partial-gradient energy bound. Seams and gradient approximation have vanishing reference cost. Thus\n\\[\n\\limsup\\operatorname{Var}(h,A_{\\mathrm{tor}}g_k)\n\\le a_*\\int_{\\mathbb T^2}|\\nabla g|^2.\n\\]\nThis proves equality when \\(a_*=0\\).</p><p>For \\(a_*&gt;0\\), first let \\(g\\) be supported inside an unwrapped torus rectangle. Pin a fixed positive-width collar around its support. Precision comparison decreases variance; the collar separates the interior from exterior. Lemma \\(9.3\\) gives the interior variance limit\n\\(a_*\\int|\\nabla g|^2\\), because the grounded inverse sends \\(-\\Delta g\\) to \\(g\\). This matches the upper bound.</p><p>For general \\(g\\), use a finite smooth partition of unity into chart-supported shifts. On each joint covariance subsequence, the upper-bound defect is positive semidefinite. Its diagonal vanishes on each summand, hence its cross terms do also. It vanishes on the sum.</p><p>For \\(f(x)=\\cos(2\\pi x_1)\\), the inverse torus Laplacian is \\(f/(4\\pi^2)\\), with gradient energy \\(1/(8\\pi^2)\\). The discrete eigenvalue multiplied by \\(n_k^2\\) tends to \\(4\\pi^2\\), and normalization error has vanishing reference cost. This proves the cosine assertion. Lemma \\(9.4\\) gives zero-interval transforms.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every smooth shift \\(g\\) defined near \\(U\\), \\( \\log\\mathbb E^{\\mathrm{free}}_{U_k,b_k} e^{(h,A_{U_k}g_k)} \\longrightarrow \\frac{a_*}{2}\\int_U|\\nabla g|^2. \\) Joint limits are centered Gaussian, and all joint …", "statement": "<p>**Free limits in a fixed rectangle — original Lemma 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(U_k\\), rescaled by \\(n_k^{-1}\\), be free rectangles converging to a fixed nondegenerate axis rectangle \\(U\\). Use the continuity-scale assumptions of Proposition \\(9\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every smooth shift \\(g\\) defined near \\(U\\),\n\\[\n\\log\\mathbb E^{\\mathrm{free}}_{U_k,b_k}\ne^{(h,A_{U_k}g_k)}\n\\longrightarrow\n\\frac{a_*}{2}\\int_U|\\nabla g|^2.\n\\]\nJoint limits are centered Gaussian, and all joint moments converge. Zero-total density smears have covariance\n\\(a_*(-\\Delta_{U,N})^{-1}\\) on mean-free functions. Face probabilities minus volume probabilities of equal total are allowed. These conclusions include \\(a_*=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the cutting and folding argument of [14, Theorem 2.4], replacing its infinite-size affine comparison by a finite macroscopic enclosure.</p><p>For affine \\(g(x)=p\\cdot x\\), tile most of \\(U\\) by squares of a small fixed macroscopic side. Every retained coefficient tends to \\(a_*\\) by Proposition \\(8\\). Release cuts and bound remaining strips by reference cost. Sending strip area to zero after \\(k\\to\\infty\\) gives\n\\[\n\\limsup_k\\operatorname{Var}(h,A_{U_k}g_k)\n\\le a_*|U||p|^2.\n\\]</p><p>For the reverse inequality, enclose \\(U\\) strictly in a fixed square \\(S\\), and partition \\(S\\setminus U\\) into finitely many positive-width rectangles, using actual discrete endpoints. The full affine observation is the sum over pieces, up to seam flows of reference cost \\(o(1)\\), and its variance tends to \\(a_*|S||p|^2\\). Cutting increases covariance; each piece has the preceding upper bound, and those bounds sum exactly to the square value. A fixed deficit on any piece would contradict saturation on \\(S\\). Thus every rectangle is affinely saturated.</p><p>Approximate a smooth or continuous piecewise smooth gradient by constant vectors on small fixed macroscopic rectangles. Seams have vanishing reference cost, and the interior error is bounded by squared \\(L^2\\)-gradient error. Hence\n\\[\n\\limsup_k\\operatorname{Var}(h,A_{U_k}g_k)\n\\le a_*\\int_U|\\nabla g|^2.\n\\]\nThe same upper bound holds for partial observations\n\\[\nJ_{H,k}(g)=\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\{x,y\\}\\in E(U_k)\\\\x,y\\in H_k}}\n(g_k(y)-g_k(x))(h_y-h_x),\n\\qquad\nH_k=\\{x\\in U_k:x/n_k\\in H\\},\n\\]\non polygonal pieces \\(H\\). Edges crossing a fixed cut have vanishing cost.</p><p>On a joint covariance subsequence, the difference between the energy upper bound and limiting covariance is positive semidefinite. Its null space is linear. Equality, called saturation, therefore passes to linear combinations, \\(L^2\\)-gradient limits, and restrictions obtained by cutting enclosing rectangles.</p><p>For the finite reflection step, work in a rectangle symmetric about a reflection line, choose a representative with one trace height zero, and condition on the full trace \\(\\mathcal T\\). For an odd saturated shift, write partial tests \\(Y_+,Y_-\\), after reflection-symmetric transport of exceptional coefficients to the line. If off-line coefficients of \\(Y_+\\) are nonnegative, those of \\(Y_-\\) are nonpositive. The subdivided quadratic law is conditionally associated, and this passes to the primary law, so\n\\(\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_+,Y_-\\mid\\mathcal T)\\le0\\).\nReflection fixes the trace and gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(Y_-\\mid\\mathcal T)\n=-\\mathbb E(Y_+\\mid\\mathcal T).\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_+,Y_-)\n=\\mathbb E\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_+,Y_-\\mid\\mathcal T)\n-\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb E(Y_+\\mid\\mathcal T))\n\\le0.\n\\]\nTransport errors vanish in reference cost. Saturation of the sum and separate upper bounds force each partial test to saturate. Since an odd shift vanishes on the line, the weak gradient of its half-space restriction is the restricted gradient; the folded shift differs from the partial lattice test only by the vanishing seam error.</p><p>For a vertical hinge at \\(t\\), start from the odd affine shift \\(x_1-t\\) on a symmetric enclosure. Transport the \\(O(n_k)\\) exceptional negative coefficients of size \\(O(n_k^{-1})\\) to the cut along bounded paths of bounded overlap, at total squared cost \\(O(n_k^{-1})\\). This gives \\((x_1-t)_+\\). Restriction, linear combinations, and energy approximation give smooth functions of \\(x_1\\), and similarly \\(x_2\\).</p><p>For a diagonal cut, put \\(Y=x_2+t\\), and use \\(F(x_1)-F(Y)\\) on a square symmetric under interchange. First require \\(F'''&lt;0\\) and positive derivatives at both end faces. Interior coefficients on \\(x_1&gt;Y\\) are nonnegative because centered second differences decrease with their center; outer-face coefficients have positive leading terms. Exceptional diagonal-strip transport again costs \\(O(n_k^{-1})\\). Thus\n\\[\n(F(x_1)-F(x_2+t))\\mathbf1_{\\{x_1&gt;x_2+t\\}}\n\\]\nsaturates. Every smooth \\(F\\) is a difference of two functions with the strict properties, by adding a sufficiently large cubic of negative third derivative and then a sufficiently large positive linear function. Rounded reflection lines change energy by \\(O(n_k^{-1})\\).</p><p>For smooth \\(g\\), choose an interval \\(I\\) containing all \\(x_1-x_2\\) in the target rectangle, and a smooth family \\(F_t\\), compactly supported in a slightly enlarged \\(I\\), with\n\\[\nF_t'(x)=-g_{12}(x,x-t).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\partial_{12}\\int_I\n(F_t(x_1)-F_t(x_2+t))\n\\mathbf1_{\\{x_1&gt;x_2+t\\}}\\,dt\n=-F_{x_1-x_2}'(x_1)=g_{12}(x_1,x_2).\n\\]\nThe difference from \\(g\\) is a sum of one-variable functions. Riemann sums converge in gradient energy, including across moving reflection lines. Hence every smooth shift saturates.</p><p>The finite comparison\n\\(\\log\\mathbb Ee^X\\ge\\operatorname{Var}(X)/2\\)\ngives the Laplace lower bound. For the upper bound, cut into cells of fixed macroscopic side \\(r\\). Each retained smooth-gradient test has reference-cost square root \\(O(r)\\), so for fixed \\(p&gt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\log\\mathbb Ee^{pX}\n=\\frac12p^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)+O_p(r^3),\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E(|X|^3e^{p|X|})=O_p(r^3).\n\\]\nThere are \\(O(r^{-2})\\) cells. Hölder and reference bounds remove seams. Send \\(k\\to\\infty\\), then \\(r\\downarrow0\\), then \\(p\\downarrow1\\). Apply to linear combinations for joint Gaussian limits; exponential bounds give moment convergence.</p><p>Approximate densities by finite Neumann eigenfunction sums and use smooth inverse-Laplacian shifts. Discrete Neumann form approximation and Poincaré bound the reference error by squared scaled \\(L^2\\)-error. Actual rectangle endpoints handle rounding. Move face probabilities to parallel strips of width \\(r\\) using flows of cost \\(O(r+n_k^{-1})\\), then let \\(k\\to\\infty\\), \\(r\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The fractional mean modulo \\(2\\pi\\) converges to uniform measure independently of the Gaussian observations of Lemma \\(9.1\\).", "statement": "<p>**Uniform fractional means — original Lemma 5.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Lemma \\(9.1\\), with \\(a_*&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an integer-total profile \\(s\\), write\n  \\(\\varphi(s)=\\mathbb Ee^{i(h,s)}\\); this is representative-independent, positive, and at most one.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u_{U_k}\\) be uniform probability on \\(U_k\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The fractional mean modulo \\(2\\pi\\) converges to uniform measure independently of the Gaussian observations of Lemma \\(9.1\\). For each fixed nonzero integer \\(l\\) and each zero-total profile \\(v_k\\) of bounded reference cost,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\exp\\{i(h,lu_{U_k}+v_k)\\}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThe joint convergence also permits fixed real tilts and polynomial insertions in the mean-free observations.</p>", "proof": "<p>The characteristic logarithmic Hessian comparison gives, for zero-total \\(v\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\sqrt{-\\log\\varphi(s+v)}-\\sqrt{-\\log\\varphi(s)}\n\\right|\n\\le\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(h,v)/2}.\n\\]\nIndeed, for \\(F(t)=\\log\\varphi(s+tv)\\), \\(V=\\operatorname{Var}(h,v)\\), one has \\(F''\\ge-V\\), \\(F\\le0\\). Optimizing the quadratic lower bound gives \\(|F'|^2\\le-2VF\\), and integration proves the inequality.</p><p>Follow the soft-joining and replica argument of [14, Theorem 2.5, (2.10)–(2.13)]. On a square of side \\(m=m_k\\), let \\(u_m\\) be volume probability, \\(\\rho_m\\) right-face probability, and\n\\[\nX=(h,A_{B_m}(x_1/m)),\\qquad\nY=(h,\\rho_m-u_m).\n\\]\nLemma \\(9.1\\) gives\n\\(\\operatorname{Var}X\\to a_*\\) and\n\\(\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)\\to a_*/2\\).</p><p>Suppose \\(\\varphi(lu_m)\\) stays bounded away from zero. Uniform exponential moments give a subsequence on which\n\\[\nF_m(t,v)=\\mathbb Ee^{i(h,lu_m)+itX+ivY}\n\\]\nconverges locally with all derivatives. The transfer inequality makes the real-argument limit strictly positive.</p><p>For the \\(N\\)-bond quadratic approximants, take independent fields and write \\(S=h^1+h^2\\), \\(D=h^1-h^2\\), with both representatives zero at the same vertex. At every subdivided vertex, \\(S\\equiv D\\pmod{4\\pi\\mathbb Z}\\). Conditional on the full common parity class, \\(S,D\\) are independent and identically distributed. If \\(V_{ij}\\) is the conditional second moment of linear observations \\(S_i,S_j\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb EV_{ij}=2\\operatorname{Cov}(h_i,h_j),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Cov}(V_{ij},V_{rs})\n=2\\kappa(h_i,h_j,h_r,h_s).\n\\]\nExpansion in the original two copies proves these identities. Differentiating the transform gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E S_iS_j\ne^{i(l(h^1-h^2,u_m)+tX(D)+vY(D))}\n=-2(F_{m,N}F_{m,N,ij}-F_{m,N,i}F_{m,N,j}).\n\\]\nReplace \\(V_{ij}\\) by its mean, with error at most\n\\(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}V_{ij}}\\). First remove subdivision at fixed primary graph, then use Gaussian fourth-moment limits. For \\(ij=XX,XY\\),\n\\[\n(\\log F)_{tt}=-a_*,\n\\qquad\n(\\log F)_{tv}=-a_*/2.\n\\]\nReflection gives \\(F_t(0,0)=0\\), hence\n\\[\nF(t,v)=F(0,v)e^{-a_*t^2/2-a_*tv/2}.\n\\]</p><p>Softly join two horizontal squares using independent Bessel seam increments and\n\\[\nH_2=(h^1,\\rho_R)-(h^2,\\rho_L)-\\bar\\eta,\n\\]\npenalized by \\(e^{-H_2^2/2}\\). Sum relative translations and quotient common translation. Exact seam matching recovers the joined rectangle. The soft variance of the sum of internal affine tests lies between its joined-rectangle variance and twice the free-square variance. The joined full affine test differs only by seam cost \\(O(m^{-1})\\); Lemma \\(9.1\\) makes the soft variance tend to \\(2a_*\\).</p><p>These comparisons do not reweight original free cells: first confine cell means with auxiliary precision \\(\\zeta\\sum_i\\bar h_i^2\\). The translation sum\n\\(\\sum_qe^{-\\zeta(\\mu+2\\pi q)^2/2}\\), divided by its Gaussian-integral scalar, tends uniformly to one as \\(\\zeta\\downarrow0\\). After soft joining, divide out the common-translation scalar; the relative sum is uniformly bounded in its representative shift. Dominated convergence with internal moments removes auxiliary confinement.</p><p>Poisson summation expresses the soft transform as a positive sum of integer sectors, with weights \\(e^{-q^2/2}\\chi_m(q)\\), where\n\\[\n\\chi_m(q)=\\exp\\{mb(\\cos(2\\pi q/m)-1)\\}\\to1\n\\]\nfor fixed \\(q\\), uniformly in the temperatures. If\n\\(F_m^{(q)}(t,v)=\\mathbb Ee^{iq(h,u_m)+itX+ivY}\\), reflection and charge reversal give\n\\[\n\\psi_q(t)\n=\\varphi(q\\rho_R+tA(x_1/m))\n \\varphi(-q\\rho_L+tA(x_1/m))\n=F_m^{(q)}(t,q)^2.\n\\]\nEach sector satisfies\n\\((\\log\\psi_q)''\\ge-2\\operatorname{Var}X\\).\nThe logarithmic Hessian of a positive sum equals the mean sector Hessian plus variance of sector first logarithmic derivatives. Hence soft variance is at most twice the free variance minus that nonnegative variance.</p><p>Differentiation under the sector sum is uniform: with \\(V=2\\operatorname{Var}X\\),\n\\[\n\\psi_q|(\\log\\psi_q)'|^2\n\\le2V(-\\psi_q\\log\\psi_q)\\le2V/e,\n\\]\nand raw second derivatives are bounded by \\(V\\).</p><p>At zero source, the normalizer lies between \\(1\\) and \\(\\sum_qe^{-q^2/2}\\). Thus the zero sector has weight bounded below and logarithmic derivative zero. The \\(q=l\\) sector also has weight bounded below by the transfer inequality from \\(lu_m\\) to a face. Its logarithmic derivative tends, up to orientation, to \\(-a_*l\\). The variance of sector derivatives is therefore bounded below by\n\\(p_0p_l(D_l-D_0)^2\\), with positive liminf, contradicting soft-variance convergence to \\(2a_*\\).</p><p>Thus \\(\\varphi(lu_m)\\to0\\), and bounded-cost phase transfers give the square assertion. For a rectangle, enclose it in a fixed-multiple square. Its mean differs from the square mean by bounded cost; then cut the complement, which decreases characteristic coefficients and leaves the rectangle phase on one free component. This proves the general assertion.</p><p>Mixed characteristic functions vanish for \\(l\\ne0\\), while \\(l=0\\) has the Gaussian limit. Fourier approximation on the circle gives independence. Uniform exponential moments permit real tilts and polynomial insertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed finite collection of bounded piecewise continuous densities \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_d\\), primary smears converge jointly, with all Laplace transforms and polynomial moments, to a centered Gaussian vector with …", "statement": "<p>**Moving-parameter pinned limits — original Lemma 5.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>This lemma replaces the profile assumptions of Proposition \\(9\\) by the following more general sequence hypotheses.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b_k\\) lie in a compact interval \\(J\\subset(0,\\infty)\\), and \\(n_k\\to\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On every fixed nondegenerate rectangle with mesh \\(n_k^{-1}\\), assume free Bessel laws at \\(b_k\\) have Gaussian Laplace and moment limits for mean-free density smears and energy-approximable weak Laplacian tests, with coefficient \\(a_*&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume fractional means modulo \\(2\\pi\\) converge jointly with these tests to an independent uniform variable.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\) be a fixed rectangle, and \\(p\\subset U\\) a nonempty finite union of aligned positive-area boxes. Resulting pieces have nondegenerate faces and positive-width passages; bounded lattice rounding is allowed. Pin every primary vertex of \\(p\\) to zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal H_p=\\{v\\in H^1(U):v=0\\text{ a.e. on }p\\},\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal E_U(v,w)=\\int_U\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla w.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(G_p\\) invert this form:\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal E_U(G_pf,v)=\\int_Ufv,\\qquad v\\in\\mathcal H_p.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed finite collection of bounded piecewise continuous densities \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_d\\), primary smears converge jointly, with all Laplace transforms and polynomial moments, to a centered Gaussian vector with covariance\n\\[\na_*\\int_U f_iG_pf_j.\n\\]\nThe densities need not have zero integral.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(n=n_k\\), \\(b=b_k\\), and index primary sites by \\(nU\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\), with permitted rounding. Density smears use \\(f_n(x)=n^{-2}f(x/n)\\), ordinary summation pairings, and probability averages normalized by full primary counts. Smooth shifts use \\(g_n(x)=g(x/n)\\); the unit-edge form \\(A_{U,n}\\) tends to \\(\\mathcal E_U\\).</p><p>First consider\n\\[\nX_{g,n}=(h,A_{U,n}g_n),\n\\]\nwhere \\(g\\) is smooth, may meet free walls, and vanishes near \\(p\\). Choose a closed neighborhood \\(V\\) of its support separated from \\(p\\), containing the primary test support for large \\(k\\). Choose a positive-width band \\(K\\) separating \\(p,V\\), with positive gaps, made of rectangular strips and clipped at free walls. Partition \\(K\\) into cells \\(d\\) of side comparable to \\(\\ell\\), bounded aspect ratio. With cell probability averages \\(h_d\\), set\n\\[\nW_{\\ell,\\lambda}(h)\n=\\exp\\left\\{-\\frac\\lambda2\\sum_d|d|h_d^2\\right\\},\n\\qquad\n\\widehat W=\\frac{W_{\\ell,\\lambda}}{\\mathcal P_n(W_{\\ell,\\lambda})},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is the free law modulo common translations, normalized there, then summed over all translations. The band confines its constant. The order is\n\\[\nk\\to\\infty,\\qquad\\ell\\downarrow0,\\qquad\\lambda\\to\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>**Band limits.** At fixed partition and strength, the assumed free and phase limits turn translation summation into integration of the constant against \\(dc/(2\\pi)\\), also with polynomial insertions. If \\(S\\) is the volume probability average and\n\\(\\bar h_K=|K|^{-1}\\sum_d|d|h_d\\), then\n\\[\nW_{\\ell,\\lambda}\\le e^{-\\lambda|K|\\bar h_K^2/2},\n\\]\nwhile \\(S-\\bar h_K\\) has bounded reference cost. Reference moments and exponential bounds control this difference; the Gaussian factor controls translations. The limiting law has Gaussian precision\n\\[\na_*^{-1}\\mathcal E_U+\\lambda\\sum_d|d|v_d^2.\n\\]</p><p>After integrating the constant, let \\(C_\\ell\\) be the covariance of the cell projection of the mean-free Neumann field, with its \\(K\\)-average removed. The inverse Neumann Laplacian is Hilbert–Schmidt, so \\(\\sup_\\ell\\|C_\\ell\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}&lt;\\infty\\). If \\(t_j\\) are eigenvalues of \\(\\lambda C_\\ell\\), the normalized \\(q\\)-th weight moment has determinant contribution bounded by\n\\[\n\\frac12\\sum_j\\{q\\log(1+t_j)-\\log(1+qt_j)\\}\n\\le C_q\\sum_jt_j^2.\n\\]\nThe constant integral is a bounded positive factor at fixed \\(\\lambda\\), and band Poincaré controls every moment of \\(S\\). Therefore, for fixed \\(q\\ge1,r\\ge0,\\lambda&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\limsup_{\\ell\\downarrow0}\\limsup_{k\\to\\infty}\n\\mathcal P_n(\\widehat W^q(1+|S|)^r)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nCell projection and form approximation give band covariance\n\\[\nT_\\lambda^{-1},\\qquad\nT_\\lambda=-\\Delta_U/a_*+\\lambda\\mathbf1_K.\n\\]\nSince \\(g\\) vanishes near \\(K\\),\n\\(T_\\lambda(a_*g)=-\\Delta_Ug\\) weakly with Neumann walls. Thus \\(X_{g,n}\\) has limiting variance \\(a_*\\mathcal E_U(g,g)\\), and bounded fourth moment uniformly for \\(\\lambda\\ge\\lambda_0&gt;0\\).</p><p>**Finite cable inputs.** Use metric-graph sign clusters [13, Section 3, Lemma 3.2] and Brownian-bridge extensions of integer-restricted Gaussian fields [1, Section 7], on finite Gaussian-chain approximants. Replace each primary edge by \\(N\\) bonds of weight \\(q_N^{j^2}\\), \\(q_N=b/(2N)\\), and attach conditional Brownian bridges. A bond is open if its bridge avoids zero. Under the unweighted sigma-finite cable measure, signs of nonzero clusters are independent and fair conditional on magnitudes and open graph. Exploration stops at the first zero or next vertex. The centered unvisited law has additional zero pins and increased stopped-segment precision.</p><p>These finite disintegration and comparison facts, and the estimates below, are [14, Lemmas 2.2, 3.4–3.5]. Remove subdivision by \\(\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\\) at each fixed primary graph, before \\(k\\to\\infty\\). Primary observables and normalizers have actual Bessel limits; no cable-cluster limit is required. In cable estimates, \\(o_k(1)\\) means zero after these successive limsups at fixed auxiliary parameters.</p><p>Uniformly for \\(b\\in J\\), fixed \\(R\\), large \\(N\\),\n\\[\n\\log\\frac{\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}q_N^{j^2}e^{2\\pi tj}}\n              {\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}q_N^{j^2}}\n\\le C_{J,R}t^2/N,\\qquad |t|\\le R.\n\\]\nGrouping opposite integers bounds the numerator increment by\n\\(Ct^2\\sum_{j\\ge1}q_N^{j^2}j^2e^{2\\pi Rj}=O(t^2/N)\\).\nA primary flow extended through \\(N\\) bonds has uniform moment cost. Pins and increased precision decrease that cost; no bare subdivided resistance is used.</p><p>Write \\(R(f)\\) for minimum squared unit-edge flow cost. After a cluster of scaled diameter at least \\(r\\) is exposed, the full-component unvisited mean has grounded cost at most \\(V_r\\). If \\(D\\) consists of band cells touched by an exposed path, and \\(\\ell&lt;r/C\\),\n\\[\nR_{\\mathrm{post}}\\left(\\sum_{d\\in D}|d|c_dh_d^U\\right)\n\\le C\\ell^2\\sum_{d\\in D}|d|c_d^2.\n\\]\nThe same holds for exploration from a filled region. Path Poincaré on the unit-edge network proves this: a spanning path anchors a positive fraction of rows or columns in a fixed cell enlargement; enlargements have bounded overlap. The preceding chain estimate transfers it to the cable posterior. A reached cell's remaining probability average has uniformly bounded centered posterior moments.</p><p>Disintegrate the sigma-finite measure as \\(\\nu_{n,N}(d\\omega)R_\\omega(d\\sigma)\\), and define\n\\[\nM_C(f)=\\sum_{x\\in C}|h_x|f_x,\\qquad\nm_C=M_C(u),\\qquad Q=\\sum_Cm_C^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\(u\\) is volume probability. Internal cable vertices carry no mass. With \\(M_C(d)=M_C(u_d)\\), the finite bin estimates are\n\\[\n\\nu_{n,N}(Q\\le4t^2)\\le C(1+t),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_{Q\\le4t^2}\\left(\\sum_CM_C(f)^2\\right)^q\\,d\\nu_{n,N}\n\\le C_q(1+t)\n\\left(t\\left|\\sum_xf_x\\right|\n+R\\!\\left(f-u\\sum_xf_x\\right)^{1/2}\\right)^{2q}.\n\\]\nFor nonnegative completed-cluster cost \\(I(C)\\), and large \\(t=t(r)\\), reinsertion gives\n\\[\n\\int\\sum_{\\operatorname{diam}C\\ge r}\nI(C)\\mathbf1_{\\{m_C\\le2t\\}}\\,d\\nu_{n,N}\n\\le\n2\\int\\mathbf1_{\\{Q\\le8t^2\\}}\n\\sum_{\\operatorname{diam}C\\ge r}\nI(C)\\mathbf1_{\\{m_C\\le2t\\}}\\,d\\nu_{n,N}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(j&gt;0\\), some \\(M&lt;\\infty\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n,N}\\bigl((1+|S|)^{-M}\n\\mathbf1_{\\{t^2&lt;Q\\le4t^2\\}}\\bigr)\n\\le C_j(1+t)^{-j}.\n\\]\nTheir constants are uniform on \\(J\\). The bin bounds use fair-sign Khintchine and Paley–Zygmund, at most \\(C(1+t)\\) translations with \\(|S|\\le Ct\\), and primary moments. Exposure anchors the complementary mean, proving reinsertion. Polynomial damping separates small clusters, controlled by mean-free box tests, from macroscopic anchoring clusters. These are [14, (3.7)–(3.9)].</p><p>**Weighted clusters and tilt errors.** For\n\\(F=(\\sum_CM_C(f)^2)^{1/2}\\), or a signless-selected signed partial sum, and a dyadic \\(Q\\)-bin \\(A_t\\), Hölder gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal P_{n,N}(\\widehat W_N|F|^q\\mathbf1_{A_t})\n&amp;\\le\n\\mathcal P_{n,N}(\\widehat W_N^4(1+|S|)^M)^{1/4}\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\cdot\n\\mathcal P_{n,N}((1+|S|)^{-M}\\mathbf1_{A_t})^{1/4}\n\\mathcal P_{n,N}(|F|^{2q}\\mathbf1_{A_t})^{1/2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nArbitrary polynomial damping controls sums and tails. Put\n\\(D_\\ell^2=1+\\log(1/\\ell)\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{W,N}\\left(\\sum_CM_C(d)^2\\right)^q\n+\\mathbb E_{W,N}|d_*|^{2q}\n\\le C_{q,\\lambda}D_\\ell^{2q}+o_k(1),\n\\]\nwhere \\(d_*\\) is any signed selection with nonnegative coefficients bounded by the full cell average. The logarithm is the reference cost of cell probability minus \\(S\\); fixed boxes have a fixed constant instead.</p><p>Replacing signs of all clusters of diameter at least \\(r\\) by independent fair signs changes the band law by vanishing total variation after the mesh and cell limits. Quantitatively, on \\(Q\\le t^2\\), expansion of the band-energy difference, fair-sign orthogonality, one-cluster exposure, local posterior cost, and reinsertion give\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n,N}\\!\\left(\n\\mathbf1_{\\{Q\\le t^2\\}}\n|\\log W(\\sigma')-\\log W(\\sigma)|^2\n\\right)\n\\le C_{\\lambda,t,r}\\ell^2D_\\ell^2+o_k(1).\n\\]\nThe intermediate moment is bounded by\n\\(C\\ell^2\\sum_d|d|M_C(d)^2\\).\nOn the finite-mass bin, split at a fixed logarithmic tolerance, use Hölder on its exceptional set and\n\\(|e^x-e^y|\\le(e^\\eta-1)(e^x+e^y)\\) when \\(|x-y|\\le\\eta\\), then let \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\). Weighted moments remove the bin cutoff, also for polynomial observables. Exchanging old and new signs preserves the unweighted measure and \\(Q\\).</p><p>Small clusters contribute at most\n\\(C_tr^2(1+\\log(1/r))\\) in second moment to bounded density smears; partition into \\(r\\)-cells and use bounded overlap. Higher moments and tails give the same vanishing. Therefore, for nonnegative fixed density smears,\n\\[\n\\limsup_{\\ell\\downarrow0}\\limsup_{k\\to\\infty}\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\n\\mathbb E_{W,N}\n\\sum_{\\operatorname{diam}C\\ge r}M_C(f_1)M_C(f_2)\n\\le(f_1,T_\\lambda^{-1}f_2).\n\\]\nFair-sign Jensen bounds all fixed moments of\n\\(Q_f=\\sum_{\\operatorname{diam}C\\ge r}M_C(f)^2\\)\nby Gaussian even moments. These bounds are uniform toward \\(\\lambda\\to\\infty\\) after mesh and cell limits, by Gaussian covariance monotonicity.</p><p>During exploration, write \\(h_d=d_*+h_d^U\\). Relative to its centered posterior with restricted band precision, the unvisited law has tilt\n\\[\nL=-\\lambda\\sum_d|d|d_*h_d^U,\n\\qquad\n\\zeta^2=C\\lambda^2\\ell^2\\sum_d|d|d_*^2.\n\\]\nFor a completed macroscopic cluster, define \\(\\zeta_C^2\\) with \\(M_C(d)\\) replacing \\(d_*\\), and \\(E_2=\\sum_{\\operatorname{diam}C\\ge r}\\zeta_C^2\\). Weighted Jensen gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{W,N}\\zeta^{2q}+\\mathbb E_{W,N}E_2^q\n\\le C_{q,\\lambda,r}\\ell^{2q}D_\\ell^{2q}+o_k(1).\n\\]\nFor \\(\\zeta\\le1\\), centered posterior \\(\\mu\\) satisfies\n\\[\n1\\le\\mu(e^L),\\quad\n\\mu(e^{2L})\\le e^{C\\zeta^2},\\quad\n\\mu((e^L-1)^2)\\le C'\\zeta^2.\n\\]\nThus fixed-cost posterior means and second moments change by \\(O(\\zeta)\\).</p><p>Polynomial truncation removes larger tilts. In size-biased exploration,\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\operatorname{diam}C\\ge r}\nM_C(B)\\mathbf1_{\\{\\zeta_C&gt;1\\}}\n(1+h_i^2+M_C(i)^2)\n\\le\\sqrt{Q_BE_2}(1+h_i^2+Q_i).\n\\]\nFor \\(\\zeta_C\\le1\\), errors are bounded by\n\\(\\sqrt{Q_B}(1+\\sqrt{Q_i})(\\sqrt{E_2}+E_2)\\).\nHölder and the preceding moments make both vanish. Exploration from a filled region is handled by discarding \\(\\zeta&gt;1\\). These are the finite tilt estimates [14, (3.12)–(3.14)].</p><p>**Vanishing reach.** Choose finite small-cell coverings \\(i,B\\) on the starting and target sides, separated from the band and from starting and target regions. They cover separator strips. Fix their radius \\(\\rho\\) before band refinement. Choose \\(r&gt;0\\) below all fixed separations, so relevant crossing clusters are macroscopic.</p><p>Path anchoring bounds centered posterior variance of a reached-cell average by \\(C_0\\), independently of \\(\\rho\\). A logarithmic trial supported away from the band gives prior continuum variance at least\n\\[\nca_*\\log(1/\\rho)-C,\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(\\lambda\\), also at Neumann free walls. Since \\(a_*&gt;0\\), choose \\(\\rho\\) so prior variance exceeds \\(C_0+2c_0\\). At fixed strength, fine partition and mesh give a discrete gap \\(c_0\\). This is the only use of positive \\(a_*\\); finite estimates require only \\(b\\in J\\).</p><p>Decomposing a target average into exposed and unvisited parts, using posterior variance deficit on reach, vanishing tilt errors, and fair-sign resampling gives\n\\[\nc_0\\mathbb P_{W,N}(B\\text{ reached})\n\\le\n\\mathbb E_{W,N}\\sum_{C:C\\cap p\\ne\\varnothing}M_C(B)^2+o(1).\n\\]\nFor a second comparison, bias by \\(|h_x|\\), \\(x\\in B\\), and expose its cluster. The finite inequality\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[X^2|Y|]\\ge\\mathbb EX^2\\,\\mathbb E|Y|\n\\]\nfor centered band precision follows from the characteristic Hessian comparison by integrating\n\\(\\mathbb E[X^2\\cos(tY)]\\le\\mathbb EX^2\\mathbb E\\cos(tY)\\)\nagainst \\(2\\,dt/(\\pi t^2)\\), after subtraction at zero. Thus pre-exposure second moment at \\(i\\) is no smaller than its unbiased variance. Completed-cluster bias cancels from the complement law. The same deficit gives\n\\[\nc_0\\mathbb E_{W,N}\\sum_{C:C\\cap i\\ne\\varnothing}M_C(B)\n\\le\n\\mathbb E_{W,N}\\sum_CM_C(B)M_C(i)^2+o(1).\n\\]\nThe preceding tilt bounds justify summed errors; these are [14, (3.16)–(3.17)].</p><p>As \\(\\lambda\\to\\infty\\), \\(T_\\lambda^{-1}\\) decreases to the inverse with hard zero band on \\(K\\), by weak energy compactness and recovery. Since \\(K\\) separates \\(B,i\\),\n\\((B,T_\\lambda^{-1}i)\\to0\\). Hence limiting cross-mass sums vanish. On \\(\\max_CM_C(i)\\le R\\), the right side of the last inequality is at most \\(R\\) times that cross sum. Its tail is controlled by\n\\[\n\\sum_CM_C(B)M_C(i)^2\\le\\sqrt{Q_B}\\,Q_i\n\\]\nand arbitrary fixed moments, uniformly in the successive limit order. Thus first-mass sums vanish. Every cluster joining \\(p\\) to \\(B\\) visits some \\(i\\). Truncate \\(\\max_CM_C(B)\\) in the first deficit inequality and control tails similarly. Therefore\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\lambda\\to\\infty}\\limsup_{\\ell\\downarrow0}\n\\limsup_{k\\to\\infty}\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\n\\mathbb P_{W,N}(\\text{exploration from }p\\text{ reaches }V)=0.\n\\]</p><p>**Hard-pin comparison and densities.** Explore every cluster meeting \\(p\\). If exploration does not reach \\(V\\), the exposed part of \\(X_{g,n}\\) is zero. The centered posterior has the primary pin \\(p\\), further zero pins, increased segment precision, and nonnegative restricted band form, so its variance is at most the primary hard-pin variance on the subdivided graph. At fixed primary graph, the latter tends to Bessel hard-pin variance. Averaged tilt errors vanish. On failure, Cauchy–Schwarz uses the bounded band fourth moment and vanishing reach probability. Thus\n\\[\n\\liminf_k\\operatorname{Var}_pX_{g,n}\n\\ge a_*\\mathcal E_U(g,g).\n\\]\nFree release gives the opposite limsup. For every real \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{t^2}{2}\\operatorname{Var}_pX_{g,n}\n\\le\\log\\mathbb E_pe^{tX_{g,n}}\n\\le\\log\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{free}}e^{tX_{g,n}},\n\\]\nwhere release is permitted because \\(A_{U,n}g_n\\) has total zero. Both endpoints tend to\n\\(a_*t^2\\mathcal E_U(g,g)/2\\). Linear combinations give joint Gaussian Laplace limits, and reference exponentials give moments. Weak free-face terms are approximated by bounded densities in reference cost while preserving the gap.</p><p>For arbitrary bounded density \\(f\\), put \\(v=G_pf\\). Smooth shifts \\(g_j\\), vanishing near \\(p\\) and allowed at free walls, are energy-dense in \\(\\mathcal H_p\\). Choose\n\\(\\mathcal E_U(v-g_j,v-g_j)\\to0\\).\nMixed-form approximation gives\n\\[\n\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}\nR_{p,n}(f_n-A_{U,n}g_{j,n})\n=\\mathcal E_U(v-g_j,v-g_j).\n\\]\nIndeed, with grounded matrix \\(A_{p,n}\\), the left expression before its limit is\n\\[\n(f_n,A_{p,n}^{-1}f_n)-2(f_n,g_{j,n})\n+(g_{j,n},A_{U,n}g_{j,n}),\n\\]\nwhose terms converge respectively to\n\\(\\mathcal E_U(v,v)\\), \\(\\mathcal E_U(v,g_j)\\), and\n\\(\\mathcal E_U(g_j,g_j)\\).\nValues on pinned sites are immaterial; representing flows may discharge into pins. No total-zero condition on \\(f\\) is imposed.</p><p>Reference bounds make smear differences vanish in every fixed moment, first \\(k\\to\\infty\\), then \\(j\\to\\infty\\). Fixed exponential bounds and Cauchy–Schwarz give the same passage for Laplace transforms. The limiting variance is\n\\(a_*\\mathcal E_U(v,v)=a_*\\int_UfG_pf\\).\nApply to linear combinations and polarize.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\sup_{\\substack{m\\in\\mathbb N:\\ \\delta_k\\log_Lm\\in I\\\\p\\in K}} \\left|\\mathbb E^{\\mathrm{free}}_{B_m,b_k}e^{\\lambda X_{m,p}}-1\\right| \\longrightarrow0. \\) The assertion is unchanged by translations or side multipliers …", "statement": "<p>**Uniform affine transforms on a zero interval — original Corollary 5.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the sequence and profile of Proposition \\(8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(I=[u,v]\\subset(0,\\infty)\\) consist of continuity points with \\(\\mathcal A=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix compact \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\), \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R\\), and\n  \\[\n  X_{m,p}=(h,A_{B_m}(p\\cdot x/m)).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sup_{\\substack{m\\in\\mathbb N:\\ \\delta_k\\log_Lm\\in I\\\\p\\in K}}\n\\left|\\mathbb E^{\\mathrm{free}}_{B_m,b_k}e^{\\lambda X_{m,p}}-1\\right|\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThe assertion is unchanged by translations or side multipliers in a fixed compact subset of \\((0,\\infty)\\), provided resulting scales remain in a fixed zero-profile neighborhood of \\(I\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Failure of uniform convergence \\(a_m(b_k)\\to0\\) would give offending \\(m_k\\) and a subsequence with\n\\(\\delta_k\\log_Lm_k\\to w\\in I\\), contradicting Proposition \\(8\\).</p><p>Square symmetry gives\n\\(\\operatorname{Var}X_{m,p}=a_m(b_k)|p|^2\\), uniformly small for \\(p\\in K\\). The reference exponential bound at \\(\\pm2\\lambda\\) gives uniform integrability, so transforms tend uniformly to one. Translations do not change the law. Fixed compact side factors shift logarithmic scale by \\(o(1)\\), and compactness gives the final assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\mathcal A(w)=a\\quad(0<w<T), \\qquad \\mathcal A(w)=0\\quad(w>T). \\) All points of these two open intervals are continuity points.", "statement": "<p>**The profile has one jump, at the passage time — original Proposition 5.9.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the extracted profile \\(\\mathcal A\\) of Proposition \\(8\\), the continuity-scale limits of Proposition \\(9\\), and\n\\[\na=8\\pi,\\qquad T=\\frac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{3\\mathcal P}}\n\\]\nfrom Propositions \\(6\\)–\\(7\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathcal A(w)=a\\quad(0&lt;w&lt;T),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal A(w)=0\\quad(w&gt;T).\n\\]\nAll points of these two open intervals are continuity points. No value at \\(T\\) is needed.</p>", "proof": "<p>First exclude a continuity value in \\((0,a)\\). Such a value, by continuity and monotonicity, gives a compact interval \\([u,v]\\) of positive length and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) whose continuity values lie in \\([\\eta,a-\\eta]\\). Choose a fixed integer stride \\(r\\) so large that, with \\(G=L^r\\),\n\\[\ncG^{2-(a-\\eta)/(4\\pi)}&gt;2.\n\\]</p><p>On one residue class modulo \\(r\\), take consecutive \\(j\\)'s crossing \\([u,v]\\) in scale \\(\\delta_kj\\), and define on cells of width \\(\\delta_kr\\)\n\\[\nF_k(t)=\\log\\frac{z_{D_0L^{j+r}}(b_k)}{z_{D_0L^j}(b_k)}.\n\\]\nChoose endpoints tending to \\(u,v\\). At almost every \\(t\\), the profile is continuous and the cell's starting size has logarithmic scale tending to \\(t\\). Lemma \\(10.1\\) gives\n\\(\\liminf_kF_k(t)\\ge\\log2\\), and its fixed-\\(G\\) estimate supplies a common lower bound. Extend by zero to a fixed enclosing interval. Fatou gives\n\\[\n\\liminf_k\\int F_k(t)\\,dt\\ge(v-u)\\log2&gt;0.\n\\]\nBut the integral telescopes:\n\\[\n\\int F_k(t)\\,dt\n=\\delta_kr[\n\\log z_{D_0L^{j_+(k)}}(b_k)\n-\\log z_{D_0L^{j_-(k)}}(b_k)].\n\\]\nSince \\(z\\le1\\), Lemma \\(10.2\\) bounds its limsup by\n\\(\\limsup_k\\delta_kr\\,o(j_-(k))=0\\), with \\(\\delta_kj_-(k)\\to u&gt;0\\). Contradiction. Every continuity value is in \\(\\{0,a\\}\\).</p><p>For \\(0&lt;w&lt;T\\), Proposition \\(7\\) gives cosine variance \\(ag\\), while Proposition \\(9\\) gives \\(\\mathcal A(w)g\\) at continuity points. Hence those values equal \\(a\\).</p><p>If a continuity point \\(w_0&gt;T\\) had value \\(a\\), monotonicity and \\(\\mathcal A\\le a\\) would make the profile identically \\(a\\) near \\(T\\). Then \\(T\\) would be continuous, and Proposition \\(9\\) at the moving exit sizes \\(D_0L^{j_e}\\), where \\(\\delta_kj_e\\to T\\), would give variance tending to \\(ag\\), contradicting the exit deficit of Proposition \\(7\\). Thus every continuity value above \\(T\\) is zero.</p><p>A monotone function constant on the continuity points of an open interval is constant throughout that interval. This proves the statement.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(c>0\\), independent of sufficiently large fixed integers \\(G\\), such that if \\(w\\) is a continuity point with \\(a_*=\\mathcal A(w)>0\\), then along every sequence \\(\\delta_k\\log_Lm_k\\to w\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Relative growth of the face phase — original Lemma 5.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\rho_m^{\\mathrm{sym}}\\) be one quarter of the sum of the four face probabilities of \\(B_m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  z_m(b)=\\mathbb E^{\\mathrm{free}}_{B_m,b}\n  e^{i(h,\\rho_m^{\\mathrm{sym}})}.\n  \\]\n  Then \\(0&lt;z_m(b)\\le1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the extracted temperature sequence and profile.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(c&gt;0\\), independent of sufficiently large fixed integers \\(G\\), such that if \\(w\\) is a continuity point with \\(a_*=\\mathcal A(w)&gt;0\\), then along every sequence\n\\(\\delta_k\\log_Lm_k\\to w\\),\n\\[\n\\liminf_k\\frac{z_{Gm_k}(b_k)}{z_{m_k}(b_k)}\n\\ge cG^{2-a_*/(4\\pi)}.\n\\]\nFor each fixed such \\(G\\), there is also \\(c(G)&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\nz_{Gm}(b)/z_m(b)\\ge c(G)\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(m\\) and all \\(b\\) in the fixed compact temperature interval.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the finite soft-joining formula [14, (2.11)]. Tile \\(B_{Gm}\\) by \\(G^2\\) free \\(m\\)-squares. The outer-face phase has cell totals \\(\\xi_i\\), supported on boundary cells, with\n\\(\\sum_i\\xi_i=1\\), \\(\\sum_i\\xi_i^2\\le C/G\\).</p><p>Choose a flow right inverse \\(P\\) on neutral cell totals, and let \\(\\rho_{ij}\\) be the face probability in cell \\(i\\) adjoining \\(j\\). Define neutral-space vector \\(H\\) by\n\\[\n(H,d)=\\sum_{\\langle i,j\\rangle}P(d)_{ij}\n\\bigl((h_i,\\rho_{ij})-(h_j,\\rho_{ji})-\\bar\\eta_{ij}\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhere seam increments are independent Bessel variables. Cell translations change \\(H\\) by the neutral projection of their constants. Penalize by \\(e^{-|H|^2/2}\\), sum relative translations, and quotient common translation. The auxiliary-confinement argument of Lemma \\(9.2\\) justifies finite precision comparisons.</p><p>Poisson summation gives positive numerator\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{q_i\\in\\mathbb Z\\\\\\sum_iq_i=1}}\ne^{-|q-\\xi|^2/2}\n\\prod_i\n\\varphi_{m,b}\\!\\left(s_i+\\sum_{j\\sim i}P(q-\\xi)_{ij}\\rho_{ij}\\right)\n\\chi_m(P(q-\\xi)),\n\\]\nup to a common scalar. Here \\(s_i\\) restricts the outer phase and \\(\\chi_m\\) is the seam characteristic. The denominator is the corresponding total-zero sum with \\(s=0\\). The reciprocal projected translation lattice is the neutral integer lattice. Exact seam matching adds precision, so the joined coefficient is at least the soft one.</p><p>For fixed \\(G\\), the denominator tends to one at a positive continuity scale: its zero sector is one, and every nonzero sector has a nonzero integer cell total, vanishing by Lemma \\(9.2\\). Gaussian weights allow dominated convergence. Fixed-flux seam characteristics tend to one, since seam averages have variance \\(O(m^{-1})\\).</p><p>Retain sectors \\(q=e_{i_0}\\) with \\(i_0\\) at distance at least \\(G/4\\) from the boundary. The radial flow [14, (2.14)] has face fluxes \\(H_{i,\\pm j}\\), constant outer flux \\(1/(4G)\\), and cell divergence \\(e_{i_0}\\). Set\n\\[\nD_{ij}=\\tfrac12(H_{i,+j}-H_{i,-j}),\n\\qquad\nS_{ij}=H_{i,+j}+H_{i,-j}.\n\\]\nThe unit-cell radial field with Neumann boundary correction gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{i,j}D_{ij}^2\\le\\frac1{2\\pi}\\log G+C,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{i,j}S_{ij}^2\\le C.\n\\]\nOne obtains it by integrating\n\\((x-x_0)/(2\\pi|x-x_0|^2)+E(x)\\) across faces, with\n\\(|E|\\le C/G\\), \\(|\\nabla E|\\le C/G^2\\). Radial energy is\n\\((2\\pi)^{-1}\\log G+O(1)\\), and face-sum errors are summable. Prescribe \\(P(e_{i_0}-\\xi)\\) as these flows and extend linearly; the selected vectors are independent.</p><p>Expand each cell's logarithmic characteristic about the symmetric four-face profile of the same total. Square symmetry kills the linear term on zero-total face perturbations. The Hessian bound gives product lower bound\n\\[\nz_m(b)\\exp\\left\\{-\\frac12\\sum_i\\operatorname{Var}(h,Y_i)\\right\\}.\n\\]\nSince \\(q_i=S_{i1}+S_{i2}\\),\n\\[\nY_i=\\sum_{j=1}^2D_{ij}(\\rho_{i,+j}-\\rho_{i,-j})\n+\\frac{S_{i1}-S_{i2}}4\n(\\rho_{i,+1}+\\rho_{i,-1}-\\rho_{i,+2}-\\rho_{i,-2}).\n\\]\nReflections make these three components orthogonal. The odd face differences have variance exactly \\(a_m(b)\\); the even component costs at most \\(C(S_{i1}^2+S_{i2}^2)\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\limsup_k\\sum_i\\operatorname{Var}(h,Y_i)\n\\le a_*\\sum_{i,j}D_{ij}^2+C\\sum_{i,j}S_{ij}^2.\n\\]\nGaussian sector weights are bounded below since \\(|e_{i_0}-\\xi|\\) is bounded, and there are at least \\(c_0G^2\\) selected sectors. Combining estimates gives the claimed growth, with prefactor independent of \\(G,a_*\\), since \\(a_*\\le a\\).</p><p>For the uniform fixed-\\(G\\) bound, retain one central sector and use reference costs instead of limiting variances. The denominator is bounded by a fixed-dimensional Gaussian lattice sum. Each fixed-flux seam characteristic\n\\[\n\\exp\\{bm(\\cos(2\\pi q/m)-1)\\}\n\\]\nhas a uniform positive lower bound for large \\(m\\), and there are finitely many seams. The product bound retains \\(z_m(b)\\), however small it is, proving the ratio estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( -\\log z_{D_0L^j}(b_c)=o(j)\\qquad(j\\to\\infty). \\) Moreover \\(z_m(b)\\ge z_m(b_c)\\) for \\(b\\le b_c\\).", "statement": "<p>**Critical lower bound for the face phase — original Lemma 5.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(z_m\\) from Lemma \\(10.1\\), with the fixed buffer \\(D_0\\) and block ratio \\(L\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n-\\log z_{D_0L^j}(b_c)=o(j)\\qquad(j\\to\\infty).\n\\]\nMoreover \\(z_m(b)\\ge z_m(b_c)\\) for \\(b\\le b_c\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\(b_c\\), [14, (2.15)], the fixed-temperature growth estimate, has coefficient \\(a=8\\pi\\). Choose \\(c_1\\le1\\) to be half its prefactor if needed. It is independent of sufficiently large fixed \\(r\\), and for \\(G=L^r\\),\n\\[\nz_{Gm}(b_c)\\ge c_1z_m(b_c)\n\\]\nbeyond a threshold depending on \\(r\\). Fix \\(r\\), and iterate along each residue class of \\(j\\) modulo \\(r\\). Finitely many initial coefficients are positive, so\n\\[\n\\liminf_{j\\to\\infty}j^{-1}\\log z_{D_0L^j}(b_c)\n\\ge r^{-1}\\log c_1.\n\\]\nLet \\(r\\to\\infty\\), and use \\(z_m\\le1\\). Temperature monotonicity of characteristic coefficients from Lemma \\(1.1\\) gives the final claim.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\liminf_k\\delta_k\\log_L\\frac1{m(b_k)}\\ge T. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Positive pinned covariance gives the lower correlation-length bound — original Proposition 6.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a sequence \\(b_k&lt;b_c\\), \\(b_k\\to b_c\\), \\(\\delta_k=\\sqrt{b_c-b_k}\\), extracted as in Proposition \\(10\\), so\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal A(w)=a\\ (0&lt;w&lt;T),\\qquad\n  \\mathcal A(w)=0\\ (w&gt;T).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The mass \\(m(b)\\) is exactly the free-box thermodynamic, then axis-separation mass of Theorem \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\liminf_k\\delta_k\\log_L\\frac1{m(b_k)}\\ge T.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(D=(0,1)^2\\), take a larger rectangle \\(U\\), and pin a positive-width rim \\(p=U\\setminus D\\). Choose unpinned vertices to be exactly the \\(n\\times n\\) bounded-face grid of the free spin rectangle \\(\\{0,\\ldots,n\\}^2\\), up to translation and bounded rounding.</p><p>This is exactly its dual height law. Pin–pin factors cancel. Edges from interior faces to the rim become exterior-face edges with multiplicities retained; corner faces have two such edges. There are no fixed boundary spins or extra periods.</p><p>Choose smooth nonzero \\(f,g\\ge0\\), supported in horizontally separated interior boxes, and form\n\\[\nF_n=n^{-2}\\sum_zf(z/n)h(z),\\qquad\nH_n=n^{-2}\\sum_zg(z/n)h(z).\n\\]\nFor \\(\\delta_k\\log_Ln_k\\to w\\in(0,T)\\), Propositions \\(9\\)–\\(10\\) give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Cov}_{b_k,p}(F_{n_k},H_{n_k})\n\\longrightarrow\na\\int_D\\int_D f(z)G_D(z,z')g(z')\\,dz\\,dz'&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe Dirichlet kernel is positive in the connected square. These nonzero-total smears are permitted by the grounded approximation of Lemma \\(9.3\\).</p><p>If the asserted lower bound failed, a subsequence and \\(0&lt;w&lt;T,\\eta&gt;0\\) would satisfy\n\\[\n\\delta_k\\log_L(1/m(b_k))\\le w-2\\eta.\n\\]\nTake \\(n_k=\\lfloor L^{w/\\delta_k}\\rfloor\\). For large \\(k\\),\n\\[\nn_km(b_k)\\ge L^{\\eta/\\delta_k}\n\\ge n_k^{\\eta/(2w)}.\n\\]\nThe smear coefficient sums are bounded. Lemma \\(11.2\\) then makes their covariance tend to zero, since an exponential in a positive power of \\(n_k\\) dominates its polynomial prefactor. This contradicts the positive pinned limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(b>0\\), \\( C_b(x)\\le e^{-m(b)\\|x\\|_\\infty}. \\) For finite \\(G\\subset\\mathbb Z^2\\), \\( 0\\le C^{(2)}_{G,b}(u,v) \\le4C_{G,b}(u,v)\\le4C_b(v-u). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Correlation inequalities with the prescribed mass — original Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For finite free spin graph \\(G\\), define\n  \\[\n  C_{G,b}(u,v)=\\langle e^{i(\\theta_u-\\theta_v)}\\rangle_{G,b},\n  \\qquad\n  C^{(2)}_{G,b}(u,v)=\\langle e^{2i(\\theta_u-\\theta_v)}\\rangle_{G,b}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C_b(x)\\) be the infinite-volume free correlation from \\(0\\) to \\(x\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(b&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nC_b(x)\\le e^{-m(b)\\|x\\|_\\infty}.\n\\]\nFor finite \\(G\\subset\\mathbb Z^2\\),\n\\[\n0\\le C^{(2)}_{G,b}(u,v)\n\\le4C_{G,b}(u,v)\\le4C_b(v-u).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Reflection makes correlations real and Fourier expansion makes them nonnegative. Ginibre inequalities give positive correlation of cosine characters and edge monotonicity; thus finite free correlations are bounded by free-exhaustion limits [18, Lemma 16]. Those limits are translation invariant by monotone exhaustion.</p><p>For \\(u,v,z\\), Ginibre gives\n\\[\n\\langle\\cos(\\theta_u-\\theta_v)\\cos(\\theta_z-\\theta_v)\\rangle\n\\ge C_{G,b}(u,v)C_{G,b}(z,v).\n\\]\nThe corresponding sine-product expectation is nonnegative. Condition on \\(\\theta_v\\), rotate it to zero, then condition on all cosine coordinates. Sine signs form a zero-field ferromagnetic Ising model with edge coupling\n\\(b|\\sin\\theta_x\\sin\\theta_y|\\ge0\\); the sign at \\(v\\) is irrelevant. Its two-point sign expectations are nonnegative. The cosine addition formula gives\n\\[\nC_{G,b}(u,z)\\ge C_{G,b}(u,v)C_{G,b}(v,z).\n\\]\nAfter exhaustion,\n\\(C_b(x+y)\\ge C_b(x)C_b(y)\\).\nThus \\(C_b(jne_1)\\ge C_b(ne_1)^j\\), and the axis mass definition gives\n\\(C_b(ne_1)\\le e^{-m(b)n}\\).</p><p>For \\(x=(p,q)\\), \\(|p|\\ge|q|\\), reflection and supermultiplicativity give\n\\[\nC_b(x)^2=C_b(p,q)C_b(p,-q)\n\\le C_b(2p,0)\\le e^{-2m(b)|p|}.\n\\]\nInterchange coordinates otherwise.</p><p>For charge two, abbreviate \\(s_x=\\sin\\theta_x,c_x=\\cos\\theta_x\\). Rotation invariance gives\n\\[\nC_{G,b}(u,v)=2\\langle s_us_v\\rangle,\n\\qquad\nC^{(2)}_{G,b}(u,v)=8\\langle c_uc_vs_us_v\\rangle.\n\\]\nConditional sine-sign positivity and \\(c_uc_v\\le1\\) make the second at most \\(8\\langle s_us_v\\rangle=4C_{G,b}(u,v)\\). Edge monotonicity finishes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniformly for \\(b\\in I\\) and sufficiently large \\(n\\), \\( |\\operatorname{Cov}(F_n,H_n)| \\le Cn^{12}e^{-cnm(b)}+Ce^{-cn^4\\log n}. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Separated height covariance — original Lemma 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d,M,c_1&gt;0\\) and compact \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G_n\\) be a free spin rectangle contained in a square of side \\(c_1n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On bounded faces, use the dual height law with exterior-face height zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  F_n=\\sum_zf_{n,z}h(z),\\qquad\n  H_n=\\sum_zg_{n,z}h(z),\n  \\]\n  with \\(\\sum_z|f_{n,z}|,\\sum_z|g_{n,z}|\\le M\\), and supports separated horizontally by at least \\(dn\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly for \\(b\\in I\\) and sufficiently large \\(n\\),\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Cov}(F_n,H_n)|\n\\le Cn^{12}e^{-cnm(b)}+Ce^{-cn^4\\log n}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the XY current loop representation [18, Section 3]. An oriented divergence-free current has weight\n\\[\n\\prod_{\\{u,v\\}\\in E(G)}\n\\frac{(b/2)^{n_{uv}+n_{vu}}}{n_{uv}!n_{vu}!}.\n\\]\nFor \\(N_e=n_{uv}+n_{vu}\\), label edge copies, orient them, and pair incoming and outgoing half-edges uniformly at each vertex. Directed paired loops have weight\n\\[\n\\prod_e\\frac{(b/2)^{N_e}}{N_e!}\n\\prod_v\\frac1{\\ell_v!},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\ell_v\\) counts incoming, equivalently outgoing, half-edges. Summing orientations introduces the binomial factor and summing pairings cancels vertex factors, recovering the current law.</p><p>Conditional on undirected paired loops, orientations are independent and fair because \\(\\ell_v=\\deg(v)/2\\) is fixed. Height integration gives\n\\[\nh(z)=2\\pi\\sum_\\gamma W_\\gamma(z).\n\\]\nThe loop-switching identity [18, Lemma 8] gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_{G,b}(\\text{a loop visits both }u,v)\n\\le2C^{(2)}_{G,b}(u,v).\n\\]\nIndeed, if \\(m_{uv}\\) counts directed pieces from \\(u\\) to \\(v\\) without internal visits to either, a visiting loop implies \\(m_{uv}\\ge1\\), while\n\\(C^{(2)}_{G,b}(u,v)=\\mathbb E[m_{uv}/(m_{uv}+1)]\\).</p><p>Choose a reference orientation per loop and independent signs \\(\\sigma_\\gamma\\). Then\n\\[\nF_n=\\sum_\\gamma\\sigma_\\gamma F_n(\\gamma),\\qquad\nH_n=\\sum_\\gamma\\sigma_\\gamma H_n(\\gamma).\n\\]\nConditional means vanish, and conditional covariance is\n\\(\\sum_\\gamma F_n(\\gamma)H_n(\\gamma)\\). A nonzero summand winds around faces in both supports; those faces lie in the loop's horizontal range. Thus the loop has diameter at least \\(dn-O(1)\\).</p><p>Let \\(N=\\sum_eN_e\\), and \\(B_n\\) be existence of a loop of horizontal diameter at least \\(dn/2\\). Since winding is bounded by loop length,\n\\[\n\\left|\\sum_\\gamma F_n(\\gamma)H_n(\\gamma)\\right|\n\\le(2\\pi M)^2N^2\\mathbf1_{B_n}.\n\\]\nThere are \\(O(n^4)\\) vertex pairs. Switching and Lemma \\(11.1\\) imply\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(B_n)\\le Cn^4e^{-cnm(b)}.\n\\]\nOn \\(N\\le n^4\\), this gives the \\(Cn^{12}\\) term.</p><p>Before zero-divergence conditioning, \\(N\\) is Poisson with mean\n\\(\\mu=b|E(G_n)|\\le C_In^2\\). Conditioning has probability at least \\(e^{-\\mu}\\), the zero-configuration probability. Therefore\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[N^2\\mathbf1_{\\{N&gt;n^4\\}}]\n\\le e^\\mu\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{Pois}(\\mu)}\n[N^2\\mathbf1_{\\{N&gt;n^4\\}}]\n\\le Ce^{-cn^4\\log n}.\n\\]\nUse the exponential moment at \\(\\log n\\): its logarithm is \\(O(n^3)\\), versus tail factor \\(e^{-n^4\\log n}\\). Pairings preserve the marginal law of \\(N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(K>0\\), all sufficiently large \\(k\\) satisfy \\( \\sup_{c_0r_k\\le\\|x\\|_\\infty\\le C_0r_k}C_{b_k}(x)\\le r_k^{-K}. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Zero coefficient on an interval gives strong decay — original Proposition 6.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the extracted sequence and step profile of Proposition \\(10\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(w'&gt;T\\), integers \\(r_k&gt;0\\) with \\(\\delta_k\\log_Lr_k\\to w'\\), and constants \\(0&lt;c_0&lt;C_0&lt;\\infty\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(K&gt;0\\), all sufficiently large \\(k\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\sup_{c_0r_k\\le\\|x\\|_\\infty\\le C_0r_k}C_{b_k}(x)\\le r_k^{-K}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(T&lt;u&lt;v&lt;w'\\), and use radii\n\\[\n\\mathscr R_k=\n\\{4^j:j\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ u\\le\\delta_k\\log_L(4^j)\\le v\\}.\n\\]\nTheir number satisfies\n\\[\nN_k=\\frac{v-u}{\\delta_k\\log_L4}+O(1),\n\\qquad\n\\frac{N_k}{\\log r_k}\\to q:=\\frac{v-u}{w'\\log4}&gt;0.\n\\]\nAnnuli \\(R&lt;|z|&lt;2R\\) are disjoint; maximal radius is \\(o(r_k)\\), so they avoid the second source \\(x\\) uniformly in the specified range.</p><p>Choose nonnegative nonzero smooth radial \\(\\chi\\), supported in \\((1,2)\\), and\n\\[\np(z)=\\chi(|z|)\\nabla\\arg z,\\qquad\nD=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}p(z)\\cdot\\nabla\\arg z\\,dz&gt;0.\n\\]\nThis form is smooth, divergence-free, and annularly supported. Choose radial stream function \\(\\psi\\), constant on complementary components, with \\(\\nabla^\\perp\\psi=p\\). Sample \\(\\psi_R(z)=\\psi(z/R)\\) on height-graph faces and take rotated discrete differences, obtaining \\(\\eta_R\\), signed toward the positive source. Then\n\\[\n\\max_e|\\eta_R(e)|\\le C/R,\\qquad\n\\sum_e|\\eta_R(e)|^2\\le C,\n\\]\nand its discrete divergence is zero.</p><p>For an affine height difference \\(\\nabla h+\\alpha\\) representing the unit twist at \\(0,x\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_e\\eta_R(e)(\\nabla h+\\alpha)_e=D_R,\\qquad D_R\\to D&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe gradient part vanishes by summation by parts. The remaining pairing is the stream-function difference at source faces times circulations \\(\\pm2\\pi\\); it is independent of the height and chosen connection path. Riemann sums also give convergence, uniformly since \\(x\\) lies outside every annulus. Fix \\(d_{\\mathrm{circ}}&gt;0\\) with \\(D_R\\ge d_{\\mathrm{circ}}\\) at large radii.</p><p>At fixed \\(k,x\\), use a finite free spin box containing the annuli. Let \\(Z_\\alpha,Z_0\\) be twisted and centered height sums with exterior height zero. Duality and deterministic tilt give, for fixed \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\),\n\\[\ne^{\\lambda\\sum_{R\\in\\mathscr R_k}D_R}C_{G,b_k}(0,x)\n=\n\\frac{Z_\\alpha\\!\\left(e^{\\lambda\\sum_R(\\eta_R,\\nabla h+\\alpha)}\\right)}{Z_0}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix a small relative cell size \\(t&gt;0\\). Cover each form's support by disjoint-vertex square primary cells of side \\(m_R=\\lfloor tR\\rfloor\\), leaving intercell edges as seam increments. For small fixed \\(t\\), all cells meeting the support lie inside the annulus, avoid sources, and are simply connected. There are \\(O(t^{-2})\\) cells and \\(O_t(R)\\) tilted seam edges per annulus.</p><p>Release endpoint constraints between cells and seams, and all remaining constraints outside cells. The tilted affine-to-centered comparison [14, Lemma 2.2(ii)] bounds the ratio by cell transforms and independent whole-edge seam transforms. Untilted outside factors have ratio at most one. On each retained cell, \\(\\alpha=\\nabla\\kappa\\); reindex \\(h'=h+\\kappa\\), removing the connection from both weight and statistic. The factor is precisely\n\\[\n\\mathbb E^{\\mathrm{free}}_{Q,b_k}\ne^{\\lambda(\\eta_R,\\nabla h)_Q},\n\\]\nwith no additional exponential. Released constants are quotiented; edge tests annihilate them. Equivalently retain vanishing common constant penalties and cancel factors before removal. No outer-box normalizer remains.</p><p>Seam squared coefficient cost is \\(O_t(R^{-1})\\). The Bessel transform gives logarithmic seam cost \\(O_{t,\\lambda}(R^{-1})\\). Replace \\(\\eta_R\\) on each cell by its value at a chosen point. Smoothness makes total squared coefficient error \\(O(t^2)+o_R(1)\\). Hölder with exponent two and Proposition \\(1\\)'s bounded-flow estimate give\n\\[\n\\sum_Q\\log\\mathbb E^{\\mathrm{free}}_{Q,b_k}\ne^{\\lambda(\\eta_R,\\nabla h)_Q}\n\\le\n\\frac12\\sum_Q\\log\\mathbb E^{\\mathrm{free}}_{Q,b_k}\ne^{2\\lambda(\\bar\\eta_{R,Q},\\nabla h)_Q}\n+C_\\lambda t^2+o_{t,\\lambda,R}(1).\n\\]\nError coefficients times fixed tilt are bounded, as required.</p><p>Every constant-cell test is a combination of the two affine square tests, with coefficient vector at most\n\\(C_\\lambda m_R/R\\le C_\\lambda t\\). Its scale is\n\\[\n\\delta_k\\log_Lm_R\n=\\delta_k\\log_LR+\\delta_k\\log_Lt+o(1),\n\\]\ninside a compact zero-profile subinterval of \\((T,w')\\) for large \\(k\\). Lemma \\(9.4\\) makes their transforms tend uniformly to one, including bounded coefficient vectors. Once \\(t\\) is fixed there are boundedly many cells per annulus; minimum radius tends to infinity. Thus uniformly over radii,\n\\[\n\\log(\\text{product of transforms per annulus})\n\\le C_\\lambda t^2+o_k(1).\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(\\lambda\\), then \\(t\\) so \\(C_\\lambda t^2\\le\\lambda d_{\\mathrm{circ}}/4\\). For large \\(k\\), the uniform error is also at most \\(\\lambda d_{\\mathrm{circ}}/4\\). Hence\n\\[\nC_{G,b_k}(0,x)\\le e^{-\\lambda d_{\\mathrm{circ}}N_k/2}.\n\\]\nThis is uniform in every sufficiently large outer spin box. Take the prescribed free-box thermodynamic limit first. Finally choose fixed \\(\\lambda\\) so that\n\\(\\lambda d_{\\mathrm{circ}}q/4&gt;K\\), obtaining the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\limsup_k\\delta_k\\log_L\\frac1{m(b_k)}\\le T. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Finite-size criterion and upper correlation-length bound — original Proposition 6.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the extracted sequence and step profile of Proposition \\(10\\), the strong decay of Proposition \\(12\\), and the mass of Theorem \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\limsup_k\\delta_k\\log_L\\frac1{m(b_k)}\\le T.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(w'&gt;T\\), and set \\(r_k=\\lfloor L^{w'/\\delta_k}\\rfloor\\). Proposition \\(12\\), with any fixed \\(K&gt;1\\), and finite-to-infinite edge monotonicity give\n\\[\nS_{r_k,b_k}\n\\le\\sum_{z\\in\\partial H_{r_k}}C_{b_k}(z)\n\\le8r_kr_k^{-K}&lt;1/2\n\\]\nfor large \\(k\\). Lemma \\(13.1\\) yields\n\\(m(b_k)\\ge(\\log2)/r_k\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\limsup_k\\delta_k\\log_L\\frac1{m(b_k)}\n\\le\n\\lim_k\\delta_k\\log_L\\frac{r_k}{\\log2}\n=w'.\n\\]\nLet \\(w'\\downarrow T\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(S_{r,b}\\le1/2\\), then \\( m(b)\\ge(\\log2)/r. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Finite-size criterion for the prescribed axis mass — original Lemma 6.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let\n\\[\nH_r=[-r,r]^2\\cap\\mathbb Z^2,\\qquad\nS_{r,b}=\\sum_{z\\in\\partial H_r}C_{H_r,b}(0,z),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\partial H_r\\) consists of vertices adjacent to a vertex outside \\(H_r\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(S_{r,b}\\le1/2\\), then\n\\[\nm(b)\\ge(\\log2)/r.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The Lieb–Rivasseau inequality [12,17], in [18, Lemma 17], states for finite \\(H\\subset G\\), \\(u\\in H\\), \\(v\\notin H\\),\n\\[\nC_{G,b}(u,v)\n\\le\\sum_{z\\in\\partial H}C_{H,b}(u,z)C_{G,b}(z,v).\n\\]\nAt fixed \\(b,r\\), take the free-box thermodynamic limit. For target \\(se_1\\), iterate in translates of \\(H_r\\) centered at the boundary point reached previously. Each step moves the center by at most \\(r\\) in supremum norm. At least \\(\\lfloor s/r\\rfloor-1\\) iterations are possible before the target may enter the next square. Translation invariance and \\(C_b\\le1\\) give\n\\[\nC_b(se_1)\\le S_{r,b}^{\\lfloor s/r\\rfloor-1}.\n\\]\nTake \\(-s^{-1}\\log\\), then \\(s\\to\\infty\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 1, "part"], [3, 1, "part"], [4, 1, "part"], [5, 1, "part"], [6, 1, "part"], [10, 9, "part"], [11, 15, "cite"], [15, 14, "part"], [16, 14, "part"], [19, 18, "part"], [20, 18, "part"], [21, 18, "part"], [22, 18, "part"], [24, 23, "part"], [25, 23, "part"], [27, 26, "part"], [28, 26, "part"], [31, 30, "part"]]});
